window.K8S_SEARCH_INDEX=[{"title":"Introduction to Kubernetes","lead":"Kubernetes is the system that runs and looks after containers for you, across many machines. You'll learn what problems it solves, what it gives you out of the box, and how it differs from Docker.","sections":[{"h":"Quick recap: containers","id":"s1","t":"A container packages an application together with everything it needs to run (libraries, runtime, settings). Unlike a virtual machine, it does not carry a whole operating system, so it starts in seconds and uses very little memory. Docker is the best-known tool for building and running containers. Containers solved the \"it works on my machine\" problem, and they suit microservices (many small services instead of one big app). But once a company runs hundreds or thousands of them, new problems appear. That is where Kubernetes comes in. This lesson assumes you already know basic Docker and containers. NoteKubernetes is a big topic, but the first step is the easy one: understanding why it exists. If you get the four problems below, you already have the foundation, and the rest builds on it. Job listings for DevOps roles almost always mention Kubernetes, so it is worth the effort."},{"h":"What is Kubernetes?","id":"s2","t":"Kubernetes (often written K8s, because there are 8 letters between the K and the s) is an open-source container orchestration platform. The one-line answer for an interview: Docker is a container platform; Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform. \"Orchestration\" means one system decides where containers run, keeps them healthy, and adjusts them as needs change. It came out of Google, which had run containers at enormous scale with an internal, closed-source system called Borg. Kubernetes is the open-source, enterprise-grade platform built from those lessons. It is now backed by a large community under the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF), which also hosts tools like Prometheus. Think of it like…An orchestra conductor. The musicians (containers) know how to play, but someone must decide who plays when, replace a musician who leaves, and add more players for a louder passage. Kubernetes is that conductor."},{"h":"Problems with plain Docker","id":"s3","t":"Containers are ephemeral: short-lived, they can die and be replaced at any time. Running them with Docker on a single server works well for learning. In production, four problems show up. 1. Single host All containers share one machine. Say you run 100 containers and container 1 suddenly eats a lot of memory. The Linux kernel must free memory by killing some process, so container 100, which has nothing to do with container 1, may die or never start. If the machine dies, everything dies. 2. No auto-healing If a container crashes or is killed, the app is unreachable until a person notices and restarts it. On a laptop that's fine; with 10,000 containers nobody can keep running docker ps to check, and customers shouldn't be your alarm system. 3. No auto-scaling Imagine a streaming site where a hit movie is released and users jump from 10,000 to a million. Someone must start more copies by hand and set up a load balancer, because users only know one URL and can't be told \"you go to this IP, you go to that one.\" 4. No enterprise features Docker is deliberately minimal. A company app needs a load balancer, firewall-style controls (allow or block certain clients, protect against denial-of-service attacks), scaling, healing and an API gateway. Plain Docker offers none of these by default."},{"h":"How Kubernetes solves them","id":"s4","t":"Docker on one host versus a Kubernetes cluster, where copies of each app are spread across several machines. Cluster, not a single host. Kubernetes joins many machines (called nodes) into one cluster, typically one or more control-plane (\"master\") nodes plus several worker nodes. If a faulty app is hurting its neighbours on one node, Kubernetes can place the affected Pod on a different node. (You can run a one-node cluster, but that's for learning, not production.) Auto-scaling. Everything in Kubernetes is described in YAML files. To handle tomorrow's festival traffic you change replicas: 1 to replicas: 10 and apply it (manual scaling). Or you use the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA): \"when CPU load passes 80%, add another copy,\" and it keeps adding as load grows from thousands to millions. Self-healing. A ReplicaSet (the newer name for the old \"replication controller\") keeps the requested number of copies alive. When Kubernetes sees a container is failing it rolls out a replacement, often before the old one is fully gone, so users barely notice. (In Kubernetes you mostly deal with Pods, which wrap containers. More in Lesson 4.) Load balancing and service discovery. Traffic is spread across the copies, and apps find each other by name instead of by changing IP addresses (see Lesson 6: Services). Safe updates. New versions roll out gradually, and you can roll back if something breaks (see Lesson 5: Deployments). Kubernetes also fills the \"enterprise\" gap, but be honest about it: it does not solve everything out of the box. By default it only gives basic load balancing (round robin, via Services and kube-proxy). Instead it is extensible: with custom resources and custom controllers, other projects plug in more. The best-known example is ingress controllers, where a load balancer like NGINX plugs in to give advanced routing. That openness, plus the CNCF community improving it every day, is why adoption keeps growing. The idea behind all of it is desired state: you describe what you want, and Kubernetes keeps working until reality matches. Try it below. Try it: what happens when things go wrong? Pick a way of running 3 copies of an app, then cause trouble. Choose a mode and press a button."},{"h":"Features at a glance","id":"s5","t":"Feature What it means for you Self-healing Failed containers are replaced without human help Scaling Change the number of copies by hand or automatically Load balancing Traffic is shared across healthy copies Rolling updates and rollbacks Release new versions with no downtime, undo if needed Configuration and secrets Keep settings and passwords outside your images Storage Attach persistent disks to containers Extensibility Add your own object types and tools Runs anywhere Laptop, your data centre, or any cloud"},{"h":"Kubernetes vs Docker","id":"s6","t":"This is not a fight: they work at different levels. Docker builds and runs one container on one machine. Kubernetes manages many containers across many machines. Kubernetes still needs a container runtime on every node to actually run containers (today usually containerd; Docker images work fine). Docker alone Kubernetes Scope Single host Cluster of nodes Restart a crashed app Manual (or simple restart policy) Automatic, and rescheduled on another node if needed Scaling Manual Manual or automatic Updates Stop and start Rolling, with rollback Learning curve Gentle Steeper, but worth it TipKubernetes is not \"hard magic.\" It is a set of building blocks, each with a simple job. The next lessons go through them one by one, starting with the architecture. Interview angle\"What is the difference between Docker and Kubernetes?\" Short answer: container platform vs container orchestration platform. Then give the four problems: single host, no auto-healing, no auto-scaling, no enterprise features (load balancer, firewall rules, API gateway), and how Kubernetes answers each. Also be ready to say Docker alone isn't used for production."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A container in your Kubernetes cluster crashes. What happens? Self-healing: Kubernetes compares the desired state (3 copies) with reality (2 copies) and creates a new one. Which sentence describes the relationship between Docker and Kubernetes best? They work at different levels. Kubernetes orchestrates containers over a cluster. What does \"desired state\" mean? Kubernetes is declarative: you state the goal, and it keeps reconciling."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source platform that orchestrates containers across a cluster of machines. It fixes Docker's production gaps: single host, no auto-healing, no auto-scaling, no enterprise features. You declare a desired state, and Kubernetes keeps reality matching it. Key features: self-healing, scaling, load balancing, rolling updates, config and storage handling. Docker runs containers; Kubernetes manages them at scale. They complement each other."}],"n":1,"slug":"01-intro-to-kubernetes"},{"title":"Kubernetes Architecture","lead":"A Kubernetes cluster has a \"brain\" (the control plane) and \"muscle\" (the worker nodes). You'll meet every component, learn what each one does, and follow a request from kubectl all the way to a running container.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New here? Lesson 1 explains what Kubernetes is and why we use it."},{"h":"Learn it by comparing with Docker","id":"s1","t":"Lists of components are easy to forget, so let's derive them. Start with what happens in Docker, then see what Kubernetes needs on top. The four things Kubernetes adds over Docker (Lesson 1) are: a cluster, auto-healing, auto-scaling, and enterprise features like advanced load balancing, security and networking. Docker. On a machine with Docker, you type docker run and a container starts. Under the hood, a container runtime does the real work. A Java app can't run without a Java runtime; likewise a container can't run without a container runtime. Kubernetes. The smallest thing you deploy is a Pod, a wrapper around your container with extra abilities (Lesson 4). Your request does not go straight to a worker machine. It always goes through the control plane first. To see why, build up from the worker. Something must keep the Pod running (Docker has an engine for that). In Kubernetes it's the kubelet. Because Kubernetes promises auto-healing, the kubelet also keeps checking \"is the Pod running?\" and reports back to the control plane if not. Something must run the container. That's the container runtime. In Docker you only get Docker's own. Kubernetes is more open: it defines a standard called the Container Runtime Interface (CRI), so containerd, CRI-O or Docker's runtime can all be used, as long as they implement it. Something must give Pods networking. Docker has its default bridge network. In Kubernetes every Pod needs an IP address, and because of auto-scaling (say, 2 replicas) traffic must be split, \"50% here, 50% there.\" That's kube-proxy: IP addressing plus basic load balancing, using the Linux iptables rules on each node. Those three (kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime) form the worker node, also called the data plane. Together they have everything needed to run an app. So why a control plane? Because a cluster with many users and many nodes needs a \"heart\": someone to decide which node gets the Pod, to receive and secure every request, to remember the cluster's state, and to keep the controllers running. That's the next set of components."},{"h":"The big picture","id":"s2","t":"A cluster is a group of machines working together. They come in two roles: Control plane (older name: master node): makes the decisions. It stores the cluster's data, decides where Pods run, and reacts to changes. Worker nodes: the machines that actually run your containers. Think of it like…A restaurant. The manager's office (control plane) takes orders, keeps the books, and assigns tables to waiters. The kitchen and floor staff (worker nodes) cook and serve the food. A cluster: one control plane makes decisions, worker nodes run the Pods."},{"h":"Control plane components","id":"s3","t":"Component Job API server The heart of Kubernetes and the only part exposed to the outside world; the worker nodes stay internal. Every request (from you, from other components) goes through it, which is also where you would add things like single sign-on or other security checks. Every request (from you, from other components) goes through it. It checks who you are, validates the request, and reads or writes the cluster data. Nothing else talks to the database directly. etcd A key-value database that is the backing store of the entire cluster: every object is saved as a key-value pair. It is what you back up and restore from. Lose it without a backup and you lose the cluster's memory. Scheduler Watches for new Pods with no node and picks the best node, based on free CPU and memory and any rules you set. Controller manager Kubernetes has built-in controllers, such as the ReplicaSet controller: if your YAML says \"2 Pods,\" it makes sure two are always running (Lesson 5). The controller manager is the program that keeps all these controllers running. Cloud controller manager (CCM) Only in cloud clusters (EKS, AKS, GKE). When you ask for a load balancer or storage, Kubernetes must turn that into the API call each cloud understands. Kubernetes can't contain logic for every cloud, so the CCM is open source: a brand-new cloud provider can add its own logic to it. On-premises clusters don't need it at all."},{"h":"Worker node components","id":"s4","t":"Component Job kubelet The agent on every node. It takes the Pods assigned to its node, makes sure they keep running, and reports problems back to the API server so the control plane can act. kube-proxy Networking: gives Pods IP addresses and basic load balancing across replicas, via iptables rules on the node (details in Lesson 6). Container runtime The software that actually runs the containers. Any runtime that follows the Container Runtime Interface works: containerd, CRI-O, and others. (Some docs leave it off the diagram, but it is always required.) NoteA Pod is the smallest thing Kubernetes runs: a wrapper around one or more containers. It gets its own lesson next-but-one: Lesson 4: Pods."},{"h":"Follow a request","id":"s5","t":"Say you run kubectl apply -f app.yaml asking for a new Pod. Step through what happens. Try it: life of a request Press Next and watch the highlighted component in the diagram above. Press Next step to begin. kubectl to API server. kubectl sends your YAML as an HTTPS request, with your credentials from the kubeconfig file. API server to etcd. After checking your identity and permissions, the API server stores the new Pod object in etcd. It has no node yet. Scheduler. It notices a Pod without a node, picks the best one, and records that choice through the API server. kubelet. The kubelet on the chosen node sees a Pod assigned to it and asks the container runtime to pull the image and start the container. Status back. The kubelet reports \"Running\" to the API server, which saves it in etcd. kubectl get pods now shows it. Afterwards the controller manager keeps watching. If the Pod disappears later and your desired state says it should exist, a controller asks for a new one and the cycle repeats. That loop is the heart of Kubernetes. Interview angle\"What are the components of Kubernetes?\" Answer in two groups. Control plane (controls the actions): API server, scheduler, etcd, controller manager, cloud controller manager. Data plane / worker node (executes them): kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime. Then explain each one's purpose, not just its name. Good practice: write these notes with a diagram of a Pod's creation and share it."},{"h":"kubectl: your remote control","id":"s6","t":"kubectl is the command-line client for the API server. It reads a kubeconfig file (by default ~/.kube/config) that says which cluster to talk to and as whom. kubectl version --client # check the install kubectl cluster-info # where is the API server? kubectl get nodes # list the machines in the cluster kubectl get pods -A # Pods in all namespaces kubectl config get-contexts # which clusters can I reach? TipEven on a managed cluster, the control plane Pods live in the kube-system namespace. Run kubectl get pods -n kube-system to see components such as kube-proxy and CoreDNS (the cluster's DNS server) running. Interview angle\"What happens when you run kubectl apply?\" Walk through API server, etcd, scheduler, kubelet, container runtime. Mention that only the API server talks to etcd."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Which component decides which node a new Pod runs on? The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to nodes. The kubelet then runs them. Where is the cluster's state (all objects and their settings) stored? etcd is the cluster's database, and only the API server talks to it. Which component runs on every worker node and makes sure Pods are running as assigned? The kubelet is the node agent that starts Pods and reports their health."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"A cluster = control plane (decides) + worker nodes (run containers). Control plane: API server (front door), etcd (database), scheduler (placement), controller manager (reconcile loops), optional cloud controller. Worker node: kubelet (agent), kube-proxy (networking rules), container runtime (runs containers). Every action goes through the API server; only it talks to etcd. kubectl is just a client that sends requests to the API server using your kubeconfig."}],"n":2,"slug":"02-architecture"},{"title":"Managing Many Clusters (kOps)","lead":"Learning clusters on a laptop is easy. Running real ones means creating, upgrading, changing and deleting them safely, often hundreds of times. You'll learn how Kubernetes is delivered in production and how a tool like kOps manages the whole lifecycle.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson assumes you know the control plane and worker nodes from Lesson 2: Architecture."},{"h":"Local clusters vs production clusters","id":"s1","t":"Tools such as minikube, kind, k3s and MicroK8s are fantastic for learning and testing. But they are development environments: usually one node, small resources, and no high availability. Their own documentation says not to use them for production. A DevOps or platform engineer's real job includes the cluster lifecycle: Create a cluster with multiple control-plane and worker nodes. Configure it: node sizes, networking, access. Upgrade Kubernetes versions with little or no downtime (a whole topic, see Lesson 16). Delete clusters that are no longer needed, to stop paying for them. (For trying things on your own machine, see Lesson 26: kind.)"},{"h":"Distributions and managed services","id":"s2","t":"Linux is open source, yet most people use a distribution such as Ubuntu, Red Hat or Amazon Linux: the same core with extras and support. Kubernetes works the same way. Companies wrap upstream Kubernetes with installers, a nicer experience and paid support: OpenShift (Red Hat), Rancher, VMware Tanzu Managed cloud services: EKS (AWS), AKS (Azure), GKE (Google) Plain upstream Kubernetes, installed and run by your own team Under the hood they are all Kubernetes. If you understand the architecture, you understand all of them; the difference is mostly who installs, supports and upgrades it. Self-managed (e.g. kOps, kubeadm) Managed (EKS, AKS, GKE) Control plane You run it on your own machines The provider runs and patches it Support Community, or your own team. The cloud only supports the VMs, not Kubernetes Provider support for the cluster itself Control Full: every setting and component Some settings fixed by the provider Cost Pay for machines; more engineering time Usually a per-cluster fee plus nodes Good for Strong platform teams, custom needs, many cheap clusters Teams who want less operational work Interview angleNobody will ask about minikube in production. Expect \"Which Kubernetes distribution do you use, and who handles upgrades?\" Be ready to say how you create, upgrade and delete clusters."},{"h":"kOps: Kubernetes Operations","id":"s3","t":"Older tools such as kubeadm can create a cluster, but a lot is manual, and upgrades or changes are awkward. kOps (Kubernetes Operations) covers the whole lifecycle: create, update, upgrade, rolling restart, delete. Think \"kubectl for clusters.\" It is most mature on AWS. Think of it like…A building contractor. You hand over a blueprint (the cluster spec). kOps compares it with what exists, builds or changes what's needed, and can tear it down when you're done. kOps keeps the desired cluster spec in a storage bucket, then builds and maintains the real machines to match."},{"h":"Hands-on outline","id":"s4","t":"Prerequisites: an AWS account, an admin machine (an EC2 instance works) with kops, kubectl and the AWS CLI installed, and AWS credentials with EC2, S3, IAM and VPC permissions. Running a real cluster costs money, so delete it when finished. 1. Create a state store. kOps needs somewhere to keep each cluster's configuration. It uses an S3 bucket (bucket names are global, so pick a unique one). aws s3 mb s3://my-kops-state-12345 --region us-east-1 export KOPS_STATE_STORE=s3://my-kops-state-12345 2. Define the cluster. A cluster name ending in .k8s.local uses built-in \"gossip\" DNS, so you don't need to own a domain. kops create cluster \\ --name=demo.k8s.local \\ --zones=us-east-1a \\ --node-count=2 \\ --node-size=t3.medium \\ --control-plane-size=t3.medium This only writes the spec to S3. Nothing is built yet, which lets you review or edit it first (kops edit cluster demo.k8s.local). 3. Build it. kops update cluster --name demo.k8s.local --yes --admin kops validate cluster --wait 10m # waits until nodes are Ready kubectl get nodes 4. Change or upgrade. Edit the spec (new Kubernetes version, bigger nodes), apply it, then replace machines one at a time: kops edit cluster demo.k8s.local kops update cluster --name demo.k8s.local --yes kops rolling-update cluster --name demo.k8s.local --yes The rolling update drains and replaces nodes gradually, so apps stay up if they have several replicas. 5. Delete. This removes the machines, load balancers and disks it created. kops delete cluster --name demo.k8s.local --yes Watch outFlag names change between kOps versions (for example --master-size became --control-plane-size). Check kops create cluster --help for your version. And always delete practice clusters, since running EC2 instances keep billing. Try it: cluster lifecycle explorer Pick a stage to see the command and what happens behind the scenes."},{"h":"Managing hundreds of clusters","id":"s5","t":"Large companies have many clusters (per team, per region, per environment). What makes this manageable: Everything is described in files. The spec lives in the state store (and can be exported to Git), so a cluster can be recreated or compared at any time. One tool, same commands for every cluster; only the name changes. Automation. Teams call these commands from CI pipelines or Terraform rather than typing them by hand. Standard versions. Fewer Kubernetes versions across the fleet means fewer surprises and simpler upgrades. Cost control. Developers test in local or short-lived clusters; expensive managed or production clusters are kept for real workloads. Other options exist (kubeadm, Kubespray, Cluster API, or eksctl for EKS, used in Lesson 17), but the lifecycle questions are always the same."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"Why shouldn't minikube be used for production? Local tools are for learning and testing. Production needs HA control planes, real capacity and proper upgrades. What is the S3 bucket used for in a kOps setup? kOps keeps each cluster's desired spec in the state store and builds the cluster from it. You run kops create cluster without --yes. What happened? Creation is a two-step process. kops update cluster --yes actually builds it."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Local tools (minikube, kind, k3s) are for learning; production needs a real distribution or managed service. Distributions (OpenShift, Rancher, Tanzu) and managed services (EKS, AKS, GKE) are Kubernetes plus installers and support. The cluster lifecycle is create, configure, upgrade, delete. kOps manages all of it from a spec in a state store. Typical flow: create cluster, update cluster --yes, validate cluster, rolling-update cluster, delete cluster. At scale: describe clusters as files, automate, standardise versions, delete what you don't use."}],"n":3,"slug":"03-managing-clusters-kops"},{"title":"Pods: Deploy Your First App","lead":"The Pod is the smallest thing you can run in Kubernetes. You'll learn what it is, how to describe one in YAML, and the essential kubectl commands to create it, look at it, debug it and delete it.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Pods are scheduled onto nodes by the control plane. If you need the background, see Lesson 2: Architecture."},{"h":"What is a Pod?","id":"s1","t":"With Docker you run a container. In Kubernetes you cannot deploy a bare container: the lowest level of deployment is a Pod, a thin wrapper around one or more containers. You still build and use the same container images; you just deploy them as a Pod. Think of a Pod as a definition of how to run a container. With Docker you pass everything on the command line: docker run -d --name nginx -p 80:80 nginx (-d runs it in the background, --name names it, -p maps a port, and you might also add -v for volumes or a network option.) In Kubernetes you write that same information in a YAML file instead. A Pod is, for a single container, almost the same thing as a Docker container; the difference is that the specification lives in a file rather than a long command. Why the extra work? Kubernetes is an enterprise platform built on declarative files and standardisation. A developer or DevOps engineer can open the Pod file in a Git repository and immediately see the image, the port the app listens on, the volumes and so on, even with thousands of containers in production. Why the extra layer? A Pod gives its containers a shared home: One IP address for the whole Pod, so containers inside can talk to each other on localhost. Shared storage (volumes) and shared network settings. One lifecycle: they are scheduled, started and stopped together on the same node. Think of it like…A Pod is a small apartment. Usually one person (container) lives there. Sometimes a helper lives there too, and they share the address, the kitchen, and the Wi-Fi. In most cases, a Pod has exactly one container. Multiple containers are for tightly coupled helpers: sidecar containers (such as a log shipper or proxy running next to your app) and init containers (which run first to prepare things). For example, if your app needs to read files or call another container, putting both in one Pod lets them share networking and storage: container A can reach container B at localhost:3000, and they can share files. Multi-container Pods are a rare, advanced case; sidecars come back with service meshes later. Kubernetes gives the Pod an IP address (via kube-proxy, see Lesson 2), not each container. You reach the app inside through that Pod IP. A typical Pod holds one container; a sidecar Pod holds two that share an IP and storage. Watch outA bare Pod does not get auto-healing or auto-scaling. If you delete it, or its node fails, nobody recreates it. In real production you rarely deploy bare Pods: you wrap them in a Deployment (or StatefulSet or DaemonSet). A Deployment is almost the same YAML with kind: Deployment and a Pod template, which is the next lesson. You still need Pods first, since a Deployment is just a wrapper around them."},{"h":"Setting up","id":"s2","t":"kubectl is the command line for Kubernetes, like the Docker CLI is for Docker. kubectl get nodes lists the machines, kubectl get pods lists Pods, kubectl get deployments lists Deployments, and so on. Install it from the official \"install tools\" page for your operating system (it's a copy-paste of a few commands), then check it: kubectl version --client You also need a cluster. Creating a real one on a cloud costs money (see Lesson 3), so for learning use a local cluster such as minikube, k3s, MicroK8s or kind. Minikube is the easiest start. (Kind, covered in Lesson 26, runs Kubernetes nodes as Docker containers and lets you create many clusters on one laptop, so it is a good next step.) These are not full production setups, but they are perfect for learning. minikube start kubectl get nodes Minikube creates a virtual machine (or uses a Docker container) and runs a single-node cluster in it. That one node plays both control plane and worker, so kubectl get nodes shows one node named minikube with status Ready. You can choose how it runs, for example minikube start --driver=docker or another driver such as VirtualBox, depending on your system."},{"h":"Your first Pod, the quick way","id":"s3","t":"This one-liner is the Kubernetes version of the docker run above: kubectl run nginx --image=nginx --port=80 kubectl get pods The first command creates a Pod named nginx running the nginx image from Docker Hub. Right after, you may see ContainerCreating (the image is downloading), then Running. This is handy for quick tests, but real teams describe objects in files, so their setup can be reviewed, versioned and repeated. Next we'll do it the file way."},{"h":"Describing a Pod in YAML","id":"s4","t":"Every Kubernetes object is written in YAML with four top-level fields: apiVersion: v1 # which version of the API this object uses kind: Pod # what type of object metadata: # identity: name, labels, namespace name: nginx-pod labels: app: nginx spec: # the desired state containers: - name: nginx image: nginx:1.27 ports: - containerPort: 80 apiVersion and kind say what you are creating. Pods are in the core API group, so just v1. (Deployments use apps/v1.) metadata holds the name and optional labels (below). spec is where you describe what you want. For a Pod, that's the list of containers: a name, an image, and the ports they listen on. Compare with the Docker command earlier: image is the image, containerPort is the port the app listens on, and name is the name. Everything in Kubernetes (Pods, Deployments, Services) is written in YAML, so learn to read it well. But nobody memorises it: senior and junior engineers alike copy an example from the official Kubernetes docs and change the values (image, ports, names). The structure stays the same. TipBookmark the official kubectl cheat sheet; even experienced engineers use it to look up commands. Don't memorise YAML. Generate a starting file with kubectl run nginx --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml, or use kubectl explain pod.spec to see every field. Try it: Pod YAML builder Fill in the fields and watch the YAML and commands update. Pod name Image Container port Label (app=)"},{"h":"Create and inspect it","id":"s5","t":"kubectl create -f pod.yaml # create from the file (apply -f also works) kubectl apply -f pod.yaml # create or update from the file kubectl get pods # list Pods: name, ready, status, restarts, age kubectl get pods -o wide # adds the Pod IP and the node it runs on kubectl describe pod nginx-pod # full details + Events at the bottom kubectl logs nginx-pod # what the container printed kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- sh # open a shell inside it kubectl delete pod nginx-pod # remove it Command When to use it get Quick status check describe Something's wrong: read the Events section (image not found, can't schedule, and so on) logs Your app crashed or misbehaves. Add -c name for a multi-container Pod exec Look around inside: check files, test connections The kubectl get pods output is the equivalent of docker ps. With get pods -o wide you'll notice the Pod has an IP such as 10.244.0.5. That address works only inside the cluster. To test it, log into a cluster node (with minikube, minikube ssh; on a real cluster, SSH to a node) and run curl 10.244.0.5. With nginx you get the welcome page. The address also changes if the Pod is replaced. Giving Pods stable addresses is the job of Services. For a quick peek from your laptop, kubectl port-forward pod/nginx-pod 8080:80 works (see Lesson 24)."},{"h":"Labels","id":"s6","t":"Labels are key-value tags you put on objects, like app: nginx or env: prod. They do nothing by themselves, but other objects use them to select Pods: Deployments and Services find \"their\" Pods by label. kubectl get pods --show-labels kubectl get pods -l app=nginx # filter by label kubectl label pod nginx-pod env=dev # add a label"},{"h":"Multi-container Pods (briefly)","id":"s7","t":"List more than one entry under containers: spec: containers: - name: app image: my-app:1.0 - name: log-shipper image: fluent/fluent-bit:3.0 Both share the Pod's IP and can share volumes. READY will show 2/2 when both are up. Interview angle\"How do you debug a Pod?\" Your two go-to commands: kubectl describe pod NAME (status, errors and events) and kubectl logs NAME (what the app printed). Interview angle\"Why does Kubernetes use Pods instead of running containers directly?\" Because some containers must share networking and storage and be scheduled together. The Pod is the unit of scheduling. Mention sidecars, and that bare Pods don't self-heal."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You delete a Pod you created with kubectl run. What happens? Self-healing needs a controller such as a Deployment, covered in the next lesson. A Pod is stuck, not Running. Which command shows the Events that explain why? describe lists Events such as \"Failed to pull image\" or \"0/3 nodes available\". What are the four top-level fields in almost every Kubernetes manifest? Which API, what kind of object, its identity, and its desired state."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"A Pod wraps one or more containers that share an IP, storage and lifecycle. One container per Pod is the norm. Manifests have apiVersion, kind, metadata and spec. Core commands: apply -f, get, describe, logs, exec, delete. describe and its Events are your first stop when a Pod misbehaves. Labels tag objects so other objects can find them. Bare Pods don't heal themselves, so next we use Deployments."}],"n":4,"slug":"04-pods"},{"title":"Deployments & ReplicaSets","lead":"A bare Pod can't heal or scale. A Deployment fixes that: you say how many copies you want and which version, and Kubernetes keeps it true, even during updates. You'll learn Deployments, ReplicaSets, scaling, rolling updates and rollbacks.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson builds on Lesson 4: Pods (YAML structure, kubectl basics, labels)."},{"h":"Container vs Pod vs Deployment","id":"s1","t":"This is a classic entry-level interview question, and if you can't answer it, interviewers assume you haven't used Kubernetes. Here is the answer in three steps. Container: you run it with a command like docker run -d -p 80:80 -v ... --network ... image, passing every option on the command line. Pod: Kubernetes' enterprise version of that. The same options go into a YAML manifest, so a Pod manifest is simply the \"running specification\" of your container(s). A Pod can hold several containers, for example your app plus a sidecar (service meshes use this) that share networking over localhost and share volumes. Deployment: a wrapper on top of Pods that adds what Pods lack: auto-healing, auto-scaling and zero-downtime updates. A Pod is just a specification for running containers. It has no auto-healing or auto-scaling. So Kubernetes' advice is: don't create Pods directly; create a Deployment. You still learn Pods, because in the end it's Pods that run. (Pods are covered in Lesson 4.)"},{"h":"Why not just use Pods?","id":"s2","t":"Imagine you run an app as a plain Pod and someone deletes it by accident, or its node crashes, or a network problem kills it. The app is gone, and customers who try to reach it find nothing. Docker had the same weakness, so where is the Kubernetes advantage? You get it only by creating the right resource. And if a million users arrive, one Pod isn't enough; you want, say, two or a hundred copies sharing the load. You need something to watch the Pods and keep the right number alive. That's a controller: a program (Kubernetes' built-in ones are written in Go) whose whole job is to make the actual state of the cluster equal the desired state written in your YAML. Anything that does this is a controller. The controller manager from Lesson 2 runs the built-in ones, and you can add custom ones such as Argo CD (more in Lesson 12)."},{"h":"The chain: Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods","id":"s3","t":"Pod: runs your container. ReplicaSet: a Kubernetes controller that keeps the number of Pods the Deployment asked for. If one disappears, it creates another. This is what actually implements auto-healing. Deployment: a high-level abstraction on top. You create just one resource and it creates the ReplicaSet, which creates the Pods. On its own it does nothing; it hands the work to the ReplicaSet. It also adds updating and rolling back versions. You manage the Deployment; it manages ReplicaSets; they manage Pods. Think of it like…A shift manager (Deployment) who hires a team lead (ReplicaSet) for each version of the staff handbook. The team lead makes sure exactly 3 workers (Pods) are always on duty. For a new handbook, the manager brings in a new team lead and retires the old one gradually."},{"h":"Desired state and self-healing","id":"s4","t":"A Deployment's manifest states the desired state: image, number of replicas. Kubernetes constantly compares it with what is running and fixes any difference. That is why a deleted Pod comes back. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: nginx-deployment labels: app: nginx spec: replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: nginx template: metadata: labels: app: nginx spec: containers: - name: nginx image: nginx:1.26 ports: - containerPort: 80 replicas: how many Pods you want. template: a Pod blueprint (the same metadata + spec you wrote in Lesson 4). selector.matchLabels: which Pods belong to this Deployment. It must match the labels in template.metadata.labels, otherwise the Deployment is rejected. kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml kubectl get deployments kubectl get replicaset kubectl get pods # names look like nginx-deployment-5d9c7b8f4-abcde kubectl delete pod <one-pod-name> # watch a replacement appear kubectl get pods -w # -w = keep watching Everything below is hands-on, so try it. Before you start, check that your cluster is clean: kubectl get all lists the Pods, Deployments, Services and so on in the current namespace, and kubectl get all -A does it for all namespaces. Notice the Pod names: the Deployment name, then the ReplicaSet's hash, then a random suffix. Delete one and a new name appears within seconds."},{"h":"Hands-on: see the controller work","id":"s5","t":"Try this on any cluster (minikube is fine). It is the best way to feel what a Deployment gives you. First, the problem. Create a plain Pod (kubectl apply -f pod.yaml), note its IP with kubectl get pods -o wide, and check it from inside the cluster (on minikube: minikube ssh, then curl POD_IP). It works. Now kubectl delete pod nginx and curl again: the app is gone, and nothing brings it back. Create a Deployment with replicas: 1 (kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml). Then run kubectl get deploy, kubectl get rs (rs is short for ReplicaSet) and kubectl get pods. You created one resource, and all three exist. Watch it heal. In one terminal run kubectl get pods -w. In another, delete the Pod. You see the old Pod go Terminating while a new one is already Running; deletion and creation happen in parallel, so the app is never without a Pod. Scale. Edit replicas: 3 in the file and run kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml again. The ReplicaSet notices the new desired state and creates two more Pods, leaving the existing one untouched. Delete any one and it is replaced, still giving you 3. That is \"zero downtime\" in action: changing replicas or losing a Pod never disturbs the Pods that are already serving. (Routing real user traffic to the Pods is the job of Services and Ingress, coming in later lessons.)"},{"h":"Scaling","id":"s6","t":"kubectl scale deployment nginx-deployment --replicas=10 # or edit \"replicas:\" in the file and run: kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml Kubernetes creates or removes Pods until the count matches. Scaling can also be automatic with a HorizontalPodAutoscaler, which adds Pods when CPU use rises."},{"h":"Rolling updates","id":"s7","t":"Change the image in the Deployment, for example nginx:1.26 to nginx:1.27: kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.27 kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment By default the Deployment uses a rolling update: it creates a new ReplicaSet, starts one or a few new Pods, waits until they are ready, then removes old ones, step by step. At every moment some Pods are serving, so users see no downtime. How many may be added or unavailable at once is controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable (each defaults to 25%)."},{"h":"Rollback","id":"s8","t":"The old ReplicaSet is kept (scaled to zero), so going back is fast. kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-deployment kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-deployment kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-deployment --to-revision=1 More release styles (blue/green, canary) are covered in Lesson 18. Try it: Deployment simulator Desired state is on the left; reality is the Pods below. Break things and watch it recover. Desired replicas: 3 Image: v1 Ready."},{"h":"Labels and selectors","id":"s9","t":"The Deployment doesn't track Pods by name. It finds them by label. Any Pod matching app: nginx counts toward the replica number. Good to know: if you hand-create a Pod with that label, the ReplicaSet may count it and delete an extra, and changing a Pod's label removes it from the Deployment's control. Use kubectl get pods --show-labels to check. Watch outDon't edit or create the ReplicaSet directly. Always change the Deployment; it owns the ReplicaSets and your edits would be lost or fight with it. Interview angleTwo common questions. (1) \"Container vs Pod vs Deployment?\" Use the three bullets near the top. (2) \"Deployment vs ReplicaSet?\" The ReplicaSet is the controller that implements auto-healing by keeping the actual Pod count equal to the desired count; creating a Deployment creates one automatically, and the Deployment adds rollout and rollback on top. Nobody will ask you to write YAML from memory (copy examples from the official docs and change the values). Expect questions like \"what is the image field?\", \"what are labels and selectors for?\" and \"what do replicas do?\" PracticeTake an example Deployment from the official Kubernetes docs or the official examples repository on GitHub (the guestbook example is a good one), replace the image with your own app, kill Pods, change replicas and watch kubectl get rs. Day-to-day work is Deployments, not bare Pods."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"You run a Deployment with 3 replicas and delete one Pod. What happens? The desired state is 3, the actual is 2, so the controller creates another. Which command undoes the last rollout? The previous ReplicaSet is still stored, so Kubernetes switches back to it. What must spec.selector.matchLabels match? The selector tells the Deployment which Pods are its own, so it must match the template's labels."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"Deployment manages ReplicaSets; a ReplicaSet keeps N identical Pods running. You declare the desired state; controllers keep reality matching it (self-healing). Scale with kubectl scale or by editing replicas. Updates are rolling by default: new Pods start before old ones stop, so there's no downtime. Roll back with kubectl rollout undo; inspect with rollout status and rollout history. Pods are matched by labels, so selector and template labels must agree."}],"n":5,"slug":"05-deployments-replicasets"},{"title":"Services: Discovery & Load Balancing","lead":"Pods come and go, and every new Pod gets a new IP address. A Service gives your application one stable address, finds the right Pods by label, and spreads traffic across them. You'll see why it exists and how it works.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson builds on Lesson 5: Deployments & ReplicaSets (self-healing and replicas) and Lesson 4: Pods (labels on Pods)."},{"h":"The problem: Pod IPs keep changing","id":"s1","t":"A Deployment keeps, say, three replicas of your app running. Each Pod gets its own IP address from the cluster network, for example 172.16.3.4, 172.16.3.5 and 172.16.3.6. Now imagine you give those three IPs to three other teams that need to call your app. Then one Pod crashes. The ReplicaSet does its job and starts a replacement, but the replacement is a new Pod with a new IP, maybe 172.16.3.8. From your side everything looks healthy: three Pods are running. From the other team's side the app is \"down\", because they are still calling 172.16.3.4. Nobody is wrong. Pod IPs are dynamic: they are handed out when a Pod is created and are gone when it dies. Self-healing alone is not enough; you need something stable in front of the Pods. The real world works the same way. When you open google.com, Google does not tell you \"use server 100.64.2.7\". You get one name, and a load balancer behind it decides which server answers. Kubernetes gives you that with a Service (short name: svc)."},{"h":"What a Service does for you","id":"s2","t":"A Service is a stable front door for a group of Pods. It gives you three things: 1. Load balancing Requests to the Service are spread across all healthy Pods behind it, so no single replica gets overloaded. 2. Service discovery The Service finds its Pods by label, not by IP. New or replaced Pods are picked up automatically. 3. Exposure Depending on its type, a Service can be reached only inside the cluster, from the node network, or from the internet. Bonus: a stable name Every Service gets a fixed IP and a DNS name, so other apps can call payments instead of an IP. Clients only know the Service. Pods behind it can die and be replaced; the label stays the same."},{"h":"Service discovery with labels and selectors","id":"s3","t":"You might ask: if the Service forwards to Pod IPs, doesn't it suffer from the same changing-IP problem? It would, if it tracked IPs by hand. Instead it uses labels and a selector. A label is a key/value tag on an object, such as app: payments. A selector is a question: \"give me every Pod that has this label\". When the Deployment creates Pods, its Pod template stamps the same label on every Pod. When a Pod dies, the ReplicaSet creates a replacement from the same template, so the replacement carries the same label even though its IP is new. The Service only cares about the label, so it finds the new Pod on its own. Scale from 2 to 20 Pods and the Service follows, with no changes to the Service. # Deployment: stamps the label on every Pod it creates spec: selector: matchLabels: app: payments template: metadata: labels: app: payments # <- the Pod label --- # Service: selects Pods that carry that label apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: payments spec: selector: app: payments # <- must match the Pod label ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8000 Watch outThe selector must match the labels in the Pod template (spec.template.metadata.labels), not just the Deployment's own labels. A one-character typo means the Service selects nothing and traffic goes nowhere, with no error message."},{"h":"Under the hood: endpoints and kube-proxy","id":"s4","t":"Two pieces make the magic work: Endpoints. Kubernetes continuously watches for Pods that match the selector (and are Ready) and keeps a list of their IP:port pairs. This list is stored as Endpoints / EndpointSlice objects. You can see it with kubectl get endpoints payments. A Pod that dies drops off the list; a new Pod is added. kube-proxy. This component runs on every node (see Lesson 2: Architecture). It watches Services and Endpoints and writes network rules on the node (by default iptables rules) that say \"traffic to the Service IP should go to one of these Pod IPs\". The Service IP, called the ClusterIP, is virtual. No network card owns it; the node's rules rewrite the destination to a real Pod. The balancing is simple (roughly round-robin or random), not \"smart\". Features like sticky sessions or path routing need something more, which is why Ingress exists. Inside the cluster, Services also get a DNS name, using the pattern <service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local. From a Pod in the same namespace you can just call http://payments; from another namespace use payments.default or the full name. Try it: Pods come and go, the Service keeps working Three Pods carry the label app=payments. Kill a Pod and watch its IP change. Then send requests through the Service. Switch the selector to a typo to see what discovery failure looks like. Service selector: Press Send 6 requests."},{"h":"Service types at a glance","id":"s5","t":"Every Service has a ClusterIP. The type field decides who else can reach it. Each type builds on the previous one. The next lesson, Services Deep Dive, runs all of them hands-on. Type Who can reach it Typical use ClusterIP (default) Only things inside the cluster Internal calls, e.g. checkout to payments NodePort Anyone who can reach the node IPs, on a port in 30000-32767 Internal company users, quick tests LoadBalancer Anyone on the internet, via a cloud load balancer Public apps on a cloud provider Think of it like…A company's front desk. Callers dial one number (the Service) and the receptionist connects them to whichever employee (Pod) is free. Employees can quit and be replaced, and the number never changes. ClusterIP is an internal extension, NodePort is a number for people inside the building, and LoadBalancer is a public phone number. Interview angle\"Why do we need Services?\" Pod IPs are dynamic, so you need stable access (discovery), traffic spread across replicas (load balancing), and a way to expose the app (ClusterIP / NodePort / LoadBalancer). Mention that discovery uses labels and selectors, and kube-proxy programs the rules."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"How does a Service keep track of which Pods to send traffic to? Pods are matched by label via the selector. Kubernetes keeps the endpoint list up to date as Pods are created and removed. You create a Service but kubectl get endpoints shows no addresses. What is the most likely cause? An empty endpoint list means no Ready Pod matched the selector. Check for typos between the Service selector and the Pod template labels. Which Service type is the default, and who can reach it? If you do not set type, you get ClusterIP, which is reachable only from inside the cluster."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Pod IPs change whenever a Pod is replaced, so never hand them out; put a Service in front. A Service gives you load balancing, service discovery and a way to expose the app, plus a stable IP and DNS name. Discovery works through labels and selectors, so replacements and new replicas are found automatically. Endpoints list the matching Pod IPs, and kube-proxy turns that into network rules on each node. Types: ClusterIP (inside), NodePort (node network), LoadBalancer (internet via cloud)."}],"n":6,"slug":"06-services-intro"},{"title":"Interview Questions, Part 1","lead":"Ten questions that come up in almost every beginner Kubernetes interview, from Docker vs Kubernetes to \"what do you do day to day?\". Try to answer each one out loud first, then open the answer and score yourself.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this page","id":"s1","t":"Read the question and answer it out loud in 30 to 60 seconds. Hesitating for a long time sounds like you do not know the topic, so practise phrasing. Open the answer and compare. Tick I got it if your answer covered the main points. For any miss, follow the link to the lesson that explains the concept in full. Your scorecard 0 / 10 Open a question, answer it first, then tick the box."},{"h":"The questions","id":"s2","t":"1. What is the difference between Docker and Kubernetes? Docker is a container platform; Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform. Docker builds and runs containers on a single machine. Kubernetes runs containers across a cluster of machines and manages them for you. Self-healing: containers are short-lived. Kubernetes replaces failed ones automatically. Scaling: add or remove replicas by hand or automatically. No single-host limit: if a node dies, Pods move to other nodes. With plain Docker, if the host dies, the app is down. Enterprise features: load balancing, and extensibility through custom resources and controllers (for example ingress controllers). Full story: Lesson 1: Intro to Kubernetes. I got it 2. What are the main components of Kubernetes architecture? Split it into the control plane (the brain) and the worker nodes (where apps run). Control plane API server: the front door; every request goes through it. etcd: the database where all cluster state is stored. Scheduler: decides which node runs each new Pod. Controller manager: runs the built-in controllers (ReplicaSet, Node, and so on). Cloud controller manager: talks to the cloud provider, for example to create a load balancer. Worker node kubelet: manages Pods on the node. kube-proxy: programs network rules for Services. Container runtime: actually runs containers (containerd, CRI-O). Follow-up you may get\"If Kubernetes does not ship Docker by default, does it not support Docker?\" Kubernetes supports any runtime that implements the Container Runtime Interface (CRI). Container images built with Docker run fine on containerd or CRI-O. You just have to install a runtime on every node, because none is built in. Details: Lesson 2: Architecture. I got it 3. What is the difference between Docker Swarm and Kubernetes? Both orchestrate containers. The difference is scale and ecosystem. Docker Swarm is very easy to install and use, but it is best for small or simple setups and has limited networking and extension options. Kubernetes suits mid-size and large organisations. It supports advanced networking (Calico, Flannel and others), has a very active community (CNCF), and can be extended with custom resource definitions and controllers when something is missing. Market reality: almost every DevOps job description asks for Kubernetes. See Lesson 12: CRDs & Custom Controllers for the extension model. I got it 4. What is the difference between a Docker container and a Kubernetes Pod? A container is one running process packaged with its dependencies. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes: a wrapper that runs one or more containers, described in a YAML manifest. Containers in the same Pod share one network namespace (they talk over localhost) and can share storage volumes. The Pod spec also carries things a bare container lacks: labels, resource requests, probes, restart policy. See Lesson 4: Pods, and Lesson 5 for why you rarely create bare Pods in production. I got it 5. What is a namespace in Kubernetes? A namespace is a logical partition inside one cluster. Companies rarely build one cluster per team, so many teams share one cluster, and each team works in its own namespace. Names only need to be unique within a namespace, so two teams can both have a Deployment called web. You can attach access rules (RBAC), resource quotas and network policies per namespace, so one team cannot touch another team's resources. It is logical isolation only: the Pods may still run on the same physical nodes. kubectl create namespace team-a kubectl get pods -n team-a kubectl get pods --all-namespaces I got it 6. What is the role of kube-proxy? kube-proxy runs on every node and implements Services at the network level. It watches the API server for Services and their endpoints and writes network rules on the node, by default with iptables (IPVS is another mode). Example: you create a NodePort Service. kube-proxy adds rules so that traffic arriving at <node-ip>:<nodePort> is forwarded to one of the Pods behind the Service. The routing is done by the Linux kernel using those rules. See Lesson 6: Services. I got it 7. What are the different types of Services? How do they differ? Three main types, each adding reach: ClusterIP (default): a virtual IP reachable only inside the cluster. NodePort: also opens a port (30000-32767) on every node, so anyone who can reach the node IPs can use the app. LoadBalancer: also asks the cloud provider (via the cloud controller manager) for a public load balancer, so anyone on the internet can reach it. There are also ExternalName and headless Services. Hands-on: Lesson 8: Services Deep Dive. I got it 8. What is the difference between NodePort and LoadBalancer? NodePort exposes the app on every node's IP and a high port. It works for people who can reach your nodes (your office network or VPC), but users must know a node IP and an odd port, and you must open that port range on the firewall. LoadBalancer builds on NodePort and adds an external load balancer with its own public address, so users outside your organisation can reach the app. It needs a cloud provider (or a tool such as MetalLB on bare metal). On a laptop cluster the external IP stays <pending>. One more reason to know the answer: LoadBalancer costs money per Service, which is why Ingress exists. I got it 9. What is the role of the kubelet? The kubelet is the agent on each worker node that manages the Pods on that node. The scheduler assigns a Pod to a node; the kubelet makes it real. It tells the container runtime to start the containers. It runs health checks (probes) and restarts containers that fail. It continuously reports Pod and node status to the API server. If a Pod is missing, controllers (like the ReplicaSet controller) see that and create a replacement, as explained in Lesson 5. I got it 10. What are your day-to-day activities on Kubernetes? This is often the first question, so a calm, simple answer sets the tone. Cover these areas honestly, using what you really do or have practised: Operate clusters: keep them healthy, set up monitoring and alerts. Deploy and release applications: manifests, Deployments, rollouts and rollbacks. Troubleshoot: help developers when Pods will not start or Services do not route traffic (kubectl describe, logs, events). Maintain: upgrade Kubernetes versions on nodes, patch packages, fix security vulnerabilities (see Lesson 3). Support: answer tickets and teach teams how Kubernetes works. I got it TipA good answer has three parts: a one-sentence definition, one or two key points, and a real example. For instance: \"kube-proxy configures iptables rules on each node. For example, when I create a NodePort Service, it routes node-IP:port to the Pods.\""},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s3","t":"Which component decides which node a new Pod runs on? The scheduler picks the node. The kubelet on that node then starts the Pod. A teammate says \"Kubernetes dropped Docker, so it cannot run Docker images.\" What is the right response? Images follow an open standard. Any CRI runtime can run them. Which statement about namespaces is correct? Namespaces are logical isolation within a single cluster, combined with RBAC and quotas."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s4","t":"Docker runs containers on one host; Kubernetes orchestrates them across a cluster with self-healing, scaling and load balancing. Control plane: API server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager, cloud controller manager. Worker: kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime. A Pod wraps one or more containers that share network and storage. Namespaces give logical isolation; ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer give increasing reach. Give short, structured answers: definition, key points, example."}],"n":7,"slug":"07-interview-part-1"},{"title":"Services Deep Dive: Hands-On","lead":"Build a Deployment, then put each kind of Service in front of it: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer and ExternalName. You'll learn what port, targetPort and nodePort mean, and break discovery on purpose to see how selectors work.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Why Services exist (stable IPs, discovery, load balancing) is explained in Lesson 6. Deployments are covered in Lesson 5. This lesson is the practical follow-up."},{"h":"Step 1: a Deployment with two replicas","id":"s1","t":"The demo uses a small web app that listens on port 8000 and answers at the path /demo. You can use any app; just know which port it listens on (look in its Dockerfile for EXPOSE or the start command). We use two replicas so we can see load balancing. Build the image. On Minikube, build it inside the cluster's Docker so the cluster can find it: eval $(minikube docker-env) docker build -t sample-python-app:v1 . Then write deployment.yaml. You never need to memorise it; copy the example from the Kubernetes docs and change the name, labels, replicas, image and port. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: sample-python-app labels: app: sample-python-app spec: replicas: 2 selector: matchLabels: app: sample-python-app template: metadata: labels: app: sample-python-app # Services will look for this spec: containers: - name: python-app image: sample-python-app:v1 imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent # use the locally built image ports: - containerPort: 8000 kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml kubectl get deploy kubectl get pods -o wide The -o wide output shows each Pod's IP. Delete one Pod with kubectl delete pod <name> and list again: the ReplicaSet creates a replacement, usually with a different IP. That is the problem Services solve. These Pod IPs only work inside the cluster. On Minikube you can test with minikube ssh and then curl -L http://<pod-ip>:8000/demo (the -L follows the app's redirect). From your own laptop, the same URL fails."},{"h":"Step 2: the three port fields","id":"s2","t":"Before writing a Service, get these three straight. Mixing them up is the number one beginner mistake. Field Meaning In our demo targetPort The port the container listens on. Where the Service sends traffic. 8000 port The port the Service itself listens on, on its ClusterIP. 80 nodePort The port opened on every node (NodePort and LoadBalancer types only). Range 30000-32767. 30007 A NodePort request passes through three ports before it reaches your app. Try it: trace a request through the ports Change the numbers and see the path. Try a nodePort outside 30000-32767, or use Break it to see what a wrong targetPort does. nodePort port (Service) targetPort App listens on Press Trace request."},{"h":"Step 3: ClusterIP (the default)","id":"s3","t":"With no type, you get ClusterIP: a stable virtual IP that works only inside the cluster. It gives you discovery and load balancing, and it is what Pods use to call each other. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: sample-python-app spec: selector: app: sample-python-app # copy from the Pod TEMPLATE labels ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8000 Or generate it with a command: kubectl expose deployment sample-python-app --port=80 --target-port=8000."},{"h":"Step 4: NodePort","id":"s4","t":"Add type: NodePort and a nodePort to open the same port on every node. Anyone who can reach a node's IP (everyone in your company network or VPC) can now use the app. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: sample-python-app spec: type: NodePort selector: app: sample-python-app ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8000 nodePort: 30007 # optional; Kubernetes picks one if omitted kubectl apply -f service.yaml kubectl get svc # NAME TYPE CLUSTER-IP PORT(S) AGE # sample-python-app NodePort 10.96.121.5 80:30007/TCP 5s minikube ip # node IP curl -L http://$(minikube ip):30007/demo # from your laptop Notice 80:30007/TCP: Service port 80 is mapped to node port 30007. A NodePort Service also has a ClusterIP, so the inside-the-cluster address still works. Use the node port number, not 80, when calling a node IP. People outside your network still cannot reach it, because they cannot reach your nodes. TipOn Minikube with some drivers the node IP is not directly reachable. Run minikube service sample-python-app --url to get a working URL."},{"h":"Step 5: LoadBalancer","id":"s5","t":"Change the type to LoadBalancer (edit the file and re-apply, or kubectl edit svc sample-python-app). On a cloud cluster (EKS, AKS, GKE), the cloud controller manager creates a real load balancer and fills in the external address after a minute or two. kubectl get svc sample-python-app # NAME TYPE EXTERNAL-IP # sample-python-app LoadBalancer a1b2c3.elb.amazonaws.com (on AWS) Anyone on the internet can now use that address. On Minikube or other local clusters nobody provides the load balancer, so EXTERNAL-IP stays <pending>. Run minikube tunnel in a second terminal to simulate it, or install MetalLB on bare-metal clusters. Watch outEvery LoadBalancer Service usually creates its own cloud load balancer and public IP, and you pay for each. For many HTTP apps, a single Ingress is the cheaper pattern."},{"h":"ExternalName: a DNS alias","id":"s6","t":"The fourth type does not select Pods at all. An ExternalName Service makes an outside hostname look like an in-cluster Service name. Pods call my-db, and cluster DNS answers with a CNAME to the real address. Handy when a database lives outside the cluster and you may move it later. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: my-db spec: type: ExternalName externalName: db.example.com"},{"h":"Experiment: break service discovery","id":"s7","t":"Proof that Services find Pods by label only: Edit the Service selector to a typo, for example app: sample-python-ap, and apply it. Run kubectl get endpoints sample-python-app. The list is empty, and curl now fails to connect, even though both Pods are healthy. Fix the selector and apply again. Within a few seconds kube-proxy updates the rules and the app answers again. Tip: always create Services with kubectl apply -f so you can edit the file and re-apply later."},{"h":"Experiment: see the load balancing","id":"s8","t":"Send several requests through the Service, then look at which Pod logged each one: for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do curl -s -o /dev/null http://$(minikube ip):30007/demo; done kubectl logs -l app=sample-python-app --prefix --tail=10 The --prefix flag adds the Pod name to each log line. You will see both Pods handled requests. (A traffic viewer such as Kubeshark can also draw this flow, showing each request going from the Service to one of the two Pod IPs.) Interview angle\"What is the difference between port, targetPort and nodePort?\" Service port = where the Service listens; targetPort = the container's port; nodePort = the port on every node (30000-32767). Be ready to say that a wrong targetPort gives \"connection refused\" while a wrong selector gives an empty endpoint list."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s9","t":"Your container listens on 8000. The Service has port: 80. What should targetPort be? targetPort is always the port the container itself listens on. On Minikube, your LoadBalancer Service shows EXTERNAL-IP <pending>. Why? The cloud controller manager creates load balancers on cloud platforms. A local cluster has nothing to do it, so the address stays pending. Which Service type has no selector and just returns a DNS alias to an outside hostname? ExternalName maps a Service name to an external DNS name using a CNAME record."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s10","t":"port is the Service's port, targetPort is the container's port, nodePort is the node's port (30000-32767). ClusterIP is internal, NodePort adds a port on every node, LoadBalancer adds a cloud load balancer. Each type includes the one before it. The Service selector must match the Pod template labels; check with kubectl get endpoints. LoadBalancer needs a cloud provider (or MetalLB / minikube tunnel locally). ExternalName is a DNS alias for something outside the cluster."}],"n":8,"slug":"08-services-deep-dive"},{"title":"Ingress, TLS & Controllers","lead":"Ingress lets many apps share one entry point and routes traffic by hostname and URL path, with HTTPS handled in one place. You'll learn why it was invented, how the Ingress resource and the ingress controller split the work, and the three ways to handle TLS.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Ingress builds on Services. If NodePort, LoadBalancer or kube-proxy are new to you, read Lesson 6 and Lesson 8 first."},{"h":"Why Services alone are not enough","id":"s1","t":"Services already expose apps, so why does Ingress exist? Teams that moved from virtual machines to Kubernetes hit two problems. Problem 1: too few features On VMs, people used load balancers like NGINX, HAProxy or F5 with rich features: path-based and host-based routing, sticky sessions, ratio-based balancing, allow/deny lists, HTTPS, a web application firewall. A Service only offers a simple, even spread of connections (the kind of balancing kube-proxy does), nothing more. Problem 2: cost Exposing a Service as LoadBalancer usually gives each one its own cloud load balancer and static public IP, billed separately. With 200 services that is 200 bills. On VMs, you ran one load balancer with one IP and routed to many apps behind it. Kubernetes' answer is the Ingress: one entry point, many routing rules. Interview angle\"Why Ingress instead of LoadBalancer Services?\" Two reasons: (1) advanced HTTP routing and TLS features that Services lack, and (2) one shared load balancer and IP instead of one paid load balancer per Service."},{"h":"Two pieces: the resource and the controller","id":"s2","t":"Ingress is split in two, on purpose. Ingress resource: a YAML object holding rules, such as \"requests for shop.example.com/cart go to the cart Service\". It is only a description. Ingress controller: a program running in the cluster that watches Ingress resources and configures a real load balancer to match. Examples: NGINX, HAProxy, Traefik, and cloud-specific ones such as the AWS Load Balancer Controller. Why split? Kubernetes cannot build in logic for every load balancer on the market. So each vendor writes a controller, and you install the one you want. This follows the same pattern as everywhere else in Kubernetes: a resource describes the wanted state, and a controller makes it real (just like kube-proxy for Services; see Lesson 2). Watch outAn Ingress resource without a controller does nothing. You can create 100 Ingresses and nothing will happen, because nobody is watching them. Install a controller first; the ADDRESS column of kubectl get ingress stays empty until one picks it up. One entry point. The controller reads the Ingress rules and routes by host and path."},{"h":"Writing an Ingress","id":"s3","t":"A basic Ingress with host and path rules. Note networking.k8s.io/v1 (the old extensions API was removed) and that pathType is required. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: shop spec: ingressClassName: nginx # which controller should handle this rules: - host: shop.example.com http: paths: - path: /cart pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: cart-svc port: number: 80 - path: /pay pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: pay-svc port: number: 80 Routing options Host-based: shop.example.com and blog.example.com go to different Services, sharing one IP. Path-based: /cart and /pay on the same host go to different Services. Wildcard hosts: *.example.com matches any single subdomain (quote the host in YAML: \"*.example.com\"). Extras via annotations: basic auth, rate limits, redirects and so on, which depend on the controller. If no rule matches, the controller answers with a 404 from its default backend. A request with the wrong Host header gets that 404 even though the Service is healthy. Try it: Ingress routing simulator These are the rules in the cluster. Pick a host and a path, then send a request and see which backend gets it. Host header Path Press Send request."},{"h":"Several controllers: IngressClass","id":"s4","t":"A cluster can run more than one controller, for example NGINX for public sites and another for internal tools. The ingressClassName field says which controller owns an Ingress. A controller ignores Ingresses of other classes. If your Ingress never gets an address, a missing or wrong class is a very common reason. To find out what a controller has actually done, check its Pod logs or generated config. For NGINX, the controller rewrites nginx.conf with a location block for every rule."},{"h":"TLS: serving HTTPS","id":"s5","t":"TLS encrypts traffic between the client and your server (that is the \"S\" in HTTPS). With Ingress you can handle it in three ways. Passthrough Termination (offloading) Re-encrypt (bridging) Client to LB Encrypted Encrypted Encrypted LB to Pod Encrypted (untouched) Plain HTTP Encrypted again Who decrypts Only the app Load balancer LB, then app LB can inspect and route by path? No (TCP level only) Yes Yes Load on the app High Low High Main risk LB cannot filter bad requests Plain traffic inside your network Most work, but safest Passthrough: the load balancer just forwards encrypted bytes. Very private, but it cannot see paths, cookies or attacks, so features like path routing or a firewall do not work. Termination: the most common setup. The controller decrypts HTTPS and forwards plain HTTP to Pods. It is simple and fast, but traffic inside is unencrypted, which is a problem if the network is not trusted. Re-encrypt: the controller decrypts, inspects and routes, then encrypts again toward the Pod. Both hops are secure and the LB can still filter, at the cost of the Pod also doing TLS work. NoteOpenShift calls these \"edge\", \"passthrough\" and \"re-encrypt\" routes. On plain Kubernetes with the NGINX controller, termination is the default; passthrough and re-encrypt are enabled with controller annotations. TLS termination in Kubernetes The certificate and key live in a Secret of type kubernetes.io/tls, and the Ingress references it by name. Keeping certificates in a Secret means the Ingress YAML can be stored in Git safely (never commit the key itself). # 1) a certificate for testing (self-signed) openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \\ -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt \\ -subj \"/CN=shop.example.com\" -addext \"subjectAltName=DNS:shop.example.com\" # 2) store it as a TLS Secret kubectl create secret tls shop-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key spec: ingressClassName: nginx tls: - hosts: - shop.example.com secretName: shop-tls # must be in the same namespace rules: - host: shop.example.com http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: cart-svc port: number: 80 A self-signed certificate makes browsers and curl complain (\"self-signed certificate\"); for testing use curl -k or pass the cert with --cacert tls.crt. In production, use a real certificate, commonly issued automatically by cert-manager with Let's Encrypt. A wildcard certificate (*.example.com) can cover many hosts with a single Secret. Interview angleExpect \"What is an Ingress controller and why is it needed?\" Answer: the Ingress resource is only rules; a controller (NGINX, HAProxy, Traefik...) watches them and configures the actual load balancer. Without a controller, nothing happens. Also be ready to compare passthrough, termination and re-encrypt. Ready to try it? Lesson 10: Ingress Live Demo installs a controller and tests these rules step by step. For the newer replacement of Ingress, see Lesson 30: Gateway API."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"You apply an Ingress YAML on a fresh cluster and the ADDRESS column stays empty. Most likely reason? The Ingress resource only holds rules. A controller must watch it and configure a load balancer. Which TLS option lets the load balancer read paths and filter requests, while keeping both hops encrypted? Re-encrypt decrypts to inspect and route, then encrypts again toward the Pod. Passthrough cannot inspect traffic. What is one cost-related advantage of Ingress over many LoadBalancer Services? Each LoadBalancer Service typically gets its own billed cloud load balancer. An Ingress shares one entry point."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Ingress adds host/path routing, TLS and other HTTP features, and lets many apps share one load balancer and IP. The Ingress resource holds rules; the ingress controller (NGINX, HAProxy, Traefik, cloud controllers) makes them real. No controller means nothing happens. Use ingressClassName to pick the controller; pathType and networking.k8s.io/v1 are required. TLS options: passthrough (private, no inspection), termination (simple, fast, plain HTTP inside), re-encrypt (inspection plus encryption both ways). Store certificates in a kubernetes.io/tls Secret and reference it from spec.tls."}],"n":9,"slug":"09-ingress-tls"},{"title":"Ingress: Live Demo","lead":"Set up Ingress on a local cluster from zero: confirm the app works through a Service, write a host and path rule, install an NGINX ingress controller, and test it. You'll also learn to read the controller's logs and fix the usual \"it does nothing\" problems.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Why Ingress exists, how controllers work and TLS are covered in Lesson 9. This lesson only walks through the setup. It reuses the Deployment and Service from Lesson 8."},{"h":"Before you start","id":"s1","t":"You need a running Minikube cluster and the sample app from the previous lesson: a Deployment with Pods labelled app=sample-python-app, listening on 8000 and answering at /demo, exposed by a Service called sample-python-app on port 80. kubectl get pods kubectl get svc sample-python-app If you deleted them, re-create both using Lesson 8. An Ingress can point at a plain ClusterIP Service; it does not need NodePort or LoadBalancer."},{"h":"Step 1: confirm the Service works first","id":"s2","t":"Always test the layer below before adding a new one. If the Service is broken, Ingress cannot fix it. curl -L http://$(minikube ip):30007/demo # only if your Service is NodePort # or, from inside the cluster: kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://sample-python-app/demo You should see the app's text back. Good: Pods are healthy and the Service finds them."},{"h":"Step 2: write the Ingress rule","id":"s3","t":"We want requests for foo.bar.com with the path /bar to reach the Service. Create ingress.yaml (start from the example in the Kubernetes docs and change the host, path and service name): apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: ingress-example spec: ingressClassName: nginx rules: - host: foo.bar.com http: paths: - path: /bar pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: sample-python-app # your Service name port: number: 80 # the Service port, not 8000 kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml kubectl get ingress NAME CLASS HOSTS ADDRESS PORTS AGE ingress-example nginx foo.bar.com 80 5s The ADDRESS column is empty, and requests would do nothing. That is expected: no controller exists yet to read the rule."},{"h":"Step 3: install an ingress controller","id":"s4","t":"On Minikube the easiest way is the built-in add-on, which installs the NGINX ingress controller: minikube addons enable ingress The controller is just a Pod, in its own namespace. Check that it is running: kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx # ingress-nginx-controller-xxxxx 1/1 Running TipOn a real cluster you install a controller from its own documentation, usually with Helm or a manifest. Search \"ingress-nginx install\" (or your chosen controller). Do this once per cluster. After that, every new app only needs its own Ingress YAML. Check the controller noticed your rule kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx deploy/ingress-nginx-controller | tail # ... Ignoring ... / successfully synced ... Ingress \"default/ingress-example\" A line showing the Ingress was synced means the controller rewrote its nginx.conf for your rule. If the Ingress was created before the controller, it may take a moment, or you can re-apply the file. Now check the address: kubectl get ingress # NAME CLASS HOSTS ADDRESS PORTS # ingress-example nginx foo.bar.com 192.168.64.11 80 The address is filled in, so the controller has picked up the Ingress. NoteWith Minikube's Docker driver on macOS or Windows, the address may stay empty or unreachable. Run minikube tunnel in a separate terminal and use 127.0.0.1 as the target IP instead."},{"h":"Step 4: make the hostname resolve","id":"s5","t":"foo.bar.com is not a real domain, so your laptop does not know where it is. In production your company owns a real domain with a DNS record pointing to the load balancer, and this step does not exist. Locally you have two options. Option A: pretend with /etc/hosts (edit with sudo; remove it later): echo \"$(kubectl get ingress ingress-example -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}') foo.bar.com\" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts curl -L http://foo.bar.com/bar Option B: no file changes, tell curl where to connect: curl --resolve foo.bar.com:80:$(minikube ip) http://foo.bar.com/bar # or just set the Host header against the IP: curl -H \"Host: foo.bar.com\" http://$(minikube ip)/bar The rule matches on the Host header, so both ways work. If you call the IP with no host header, you get a 404 from NGINX, which is exactly what the rule is supposed to do. Watch out about pathsThe rule forwards /bar as-is to your app. Our sample app serves /demo, not /bar, so the app itself may return its own 404. To see the app's page, either set the rule path to /demo or add the NGINX annotation nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /demo. Telling \"Ingress 404\" from \"app 404\" matters: the NGINX 404 page says nginx at the bottom."},{"h":"Step 5: try more rules","id":"s6","t":"Edit ingress.yaml and re-apply to explore what Lesson 9 described: Path-based: add a second path such as /second pointing at another Service. Wildcard host: use host: \"*.bar.com\", then curl -H \"Host: example.bar.com\" ... also works. Quote the value. TLS: create a TLS Secret and add a tls: block as shown in Lesson 9. Try it: Ingress troubleshooter Pick what you see. The tool suggests the cause and the command to confirm it. Symptom Choose a symptom and press Diagnose."},{"h":"Debug checklist","id":"s7","t":"kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx: is the controller running? kubectl get ingress and kubectl describe ingress ingress-example: is the class right, is there an address, and what backends does it show? kubectl get endpoints sample-python-app: does the Service have Pod IPs? (Empty means a selector problem, see Lesson 6.) kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx deploy/ingress-nginx-controller: did the controller pick up the Ingress, and does it log your request?"},{"h":"Clean up","id":"s8","t":"kubectl delete -f ingress.yaml sudo sed -i '/foo.bar.com/d' /etc/hosts # if you edited it (use sed -i '' on macOS) minikube addons disable ingress"},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s9","t":"You applied an Ingress but ADDRESS is empty and requests fail. What should you check first? Ingress rules do nothing until a controller with a matching class reads them. In the Ingress backend, which port number goes in port.number? The Ingress sends traffic to the Service, so it uses the Service port. The Service then forwards to targetPort. Why do you edit /etc/hosts in this demo but not in production? DNS normally maps your real domain to the load balancer. The hosts file fakes that mapping on your laptop."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s10","t":"Test the Service first, then add the Ingress. Ingress points at a Service name and Service port. An Ingress has no effect until a controller is installed; on Minikube: minikube addons enable ingress. A filled ADDRESS and a \"synced\" log line show the controller picked up your rule. Local testing needs a hosts-file entry or curl --resolve / a Host header; production uses real DNS. Debug in order: controller Pod, Ingress, endpoints, controller logs."}],"n":10,"slug":"10-ingress-demo"},{"title":"RBAC: Who Can Do What","lead":"On your laptop cluster you are the boss. In a company, many people and many apps share one cluster, and each should only touch what they need. RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) is how Kubernetes decides who may do what.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"RBAC is enforced by the API server, the front door of the cluster. See Lesson 2: Architecture if you need a refresher on it."},{"h":"Why RBAC matters","id":"s1","t":"Imagine a cluster shared by developers, QA engineers and DevOps engineers. You do not want a QA engineer deleting things in the kube-system namespace, or a buggy app wiping out Deployments. Because RBAC is directly about security, a small mistake can be both dangerous and hard to debug. RBAC answers two questions, for two kinds of \"callers\": Users (humans): what may a developer, a tester or an admin do? Service accounts (programs running in Pods): what may this application do when it talks to the API server?"},{"h":"Authentication comes first, then authorization","id":"s2","t":"Every request to the API server goes through two checks in order: Authentication asks \"Who are you?\" (a user, a group or a service account). Authorization asks \"Are you allowed to do this?\" RBAC is the most common way to answer. RBAC only handles step 2. It never proves who you are."},{"h":"Where do users come from?","id":"s3","t":"This surprises many beginners: Kubernetes has no \"create user\" command. There is nothing like useradd. Instead, Kubernetes hands user management to an identity provider, just like an app that lets you \"Log in with Google\" without creating a new account. On EKS, you can map AWS IAM users and groups to Kubernetes. On OpenShift or other platforms, the provider could be LDAP, Okta, GitHub (often through a broker such as Keycloak), or any single sign-on system. The cluster receives the person's username and group names from the provider, and RBAC rules refer to those names. Service accounts are different: they are Kubernetes objects, and anyone with permission can create one."},{"h":"Service accounts","id":"s4","t":"A ServiceAccount is an identity for a Pod. When your app calls the API server (to read a ConfigMap, list Pods, and so on), it proves who it is with its service account. Every namespace has a default service account. A Pod that does not name one gets default automatically. For real apps, create a dedicated one with only the permissions that app needs. apiVersion: v1 kind: ServiceAccount metadata: name: reader-sa namespace: dev apiVersion: v1 kind: Pod metadata: name: my-app namespace: dev spec: serviceAccountName: reader-sa containers: - name: app image: nginx"},{"h":"The building blocks","id":"s5","t":"Permissions are built from two ideas: a rule set (what is allowed) and a binding (who gets it). Object Scope What it does Role One namespace Lists allowed actions on resources in that namespace ClusterRole Whole cluster Same, but also covers cluster-wide resources (like Nodes) or all namespaces RoleBinding One namespace Attaches a Role (or ClusterRole) to users, groups or service accounts in that namespace ClusterRoleBinding Whole cluster Attaches a ClusterRole to subjects everywhere Think of it like…A Role is a key card design (\"opens the supply room and the printer room\"). A RoleBinding is the act of handing that card to a specific person. A key card with nobody holding it opens nothing, and a person with no card opens nothing either. You need both. If you know AWS, a Role is close to an IAM policy. A binding connects who (subject) with what (role)."},{"h":"Writing a Role and a RoleBinding","id":"s6","t":"A Role contains rules. Each rule says which API groups, resources and verbs are allowed. Everything not listed is denied; RBAC has only allow rules. apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: pod-reader namespace: dev rules: - apiGroups: [\"\"] # \"\" means the core group (Pods, ConfigMaps, Secrets...) resources: [\"pods\", \"pods/log\"] verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\"] Now bind it to a user and a service account: apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: RoleBinding metadata: name: read-pods namespace: dev subjects: - kind: User name: priya apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io - kind: ServiceAccount name: reader-sa namespace: dev roleRef: kind: Role name: pod-reader apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io Common verbs are get, list, watch, create, update, patch and delete. For a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding, the YAML looks the same: drop namespace and change the kind. TipA ClusterRole can be reused inside one namespace. Bind it with a RoleBinding and its power is limited to that namespace. Kubernetes' built-in view, edit and admin ClusterRoles are used this way all the time."},{"h":"Testing permissions with kubectl auth can-i","id":"s7","t":"You do not need to log in as someone to test their access. Ask the API server: # Can I (the current user) delete deployments in dev? kubectl auth can-i delete deployments -n dev # Can user priya list pods in dev? (needs admin rights to impersonate) kubectl auth can-i list pods -n dev --as priya # Can the service account read secrets? kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n dev \\ --as system:serviceaccount:dev:reader-sa # Everything I may do in a namespace kubectl auth can-i --list -n dev The answer is simply yes or no. This is the quickest way to debug a \"forbidden\" error. Try it: can-i simulator Two bindings exist in the dev namespace: priya and reader-sa have the pod-reader Role (get, list, watch on pods). The user admin has a ClusterRoleBinding to cluster-admin. Pick a request and see what the API server says. Who Verb Resource Namespace Choose a request, then press Check. Watch outDo not give cluster-admin to everyone, and do not grant get secrets casually. A person who can read Secrets can read every password in that namespace. Follow least privilege: start with nothing and add only what is needed. More hardening ideas are in Lesson 23: Securing Kubernetes, and Secrets themselves are covered in Lesson 13. Interview angle\"What is the difference between a Role and a ClusterRole?\" A Role is namespaced; a ClusterRole is cluster-wide and can also cover non-namespaced resources like Nodes. \"Where do users come from?\" Kubernetes does not store users; an external identity provider supplies them, while service accounts are native objects."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"You created a Role that allows listing Pods, but the user still gets \"forbidden\". What is most likely missing? A Role only describes permissions. Nobody has them until a RoleBinding links the Role to a subject. How do you create a human user in Kubernetes? Kubernetes offloads user management. Only service accounts are created inside the cluster. Which command checks whether a service account may read Secrets? auth can-i with --as impersonates the subject and returns yes or no."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"RBAC is the authorization step: it decides what an already-identified user or service account may do. Kubernetes does not manage human users; identity providers do. Service accounts are native objects used by Pods. Role (namespace) or ClusterRole (cluster) = the permissions. RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding = who gets them. RBAC has only allow rules; anything not granted is denied. Use dedicated service accounts and least privilege; test with kubectl auth can-i."}],"n":11,"slug":"11-rbac"},{"title":"Custom Resources & Controllers","lead":"Kubernetes ships with objects like Pod, Deployment and Service. But you can teach it brand-new object types and write a program that makes them do real work. That is how tools like Istio and Argo CD plug into Kubernetes.","sections":[{"h":"The problem: built-in objects are not enough","id":"s1","t":"Out of the box Kubernetes knows a fixed list of resource types (Pod, Deployment, Service, ConfigMap, Secret and more). Suppose a project wants to add something Kubernetes does not offer, such as GitOps deployments, a service mesh, or security scanning. It cannot just tell users to \"write some YAML\" because Kubernetes would reject unknown kinds. The answer is to extend the Kubernetes API. This takes three pieces that work together. 1. CRD Custom Resource Definition. The \"blueprint\". It tells the API server a new type exists and which fields it may have. 2. CR Custom Resource. One actual object of that new type, written by a user, like one Deployment is an instance of the Deployment type. 3. Custom controller A program running in the cluster that watches those custom resources and acts on them. Without it, the resource is just stored data. Think of it like…A CRD is a new form template pinned up at the town office. A custom resource is one filled-in form. The controller is the clerk who reads submitted forms and does the work. Forms without a clerk just pile up."},{"h":"Comparing with a built-in object","id":"s2","t":"The easiest way to understand all this is to compare it with something you already know. Built-in (Deployment) Custom (e.g. Istio VirtualService) Definition of the type Already inside Kubernetes You install a CRD Your object A Deployment YAML A custom resource YAML Validation API server checks it against the built-in schema API server checks it against the CRD's schema Who acts on it Built-in Deployment controller A custom controller you install Remember: if you add an unknown field to a Deployment, the API server complains that the field is unknown. A CRD gives custom objects the same kind of checking."},{"h":"The CRD","id":"s3","t":"A CRD is just a YAML file. Here is a small one that defines a Website type: apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1 kind: CustomResourceDefinition metadata: name: websites.example.com # must be plural.group spec: group: example.com scope: Namespaced names: kind: Website plural: websites singular: website shortNames: [ws] versions: - name: v1 served: true storage: true schema: openAPIV3Schema: type: object properties: spec: type: object properties: image: type: string replicas: type: integer minimum: 1 After kubectl apply -f crd.yaml, the API server has a new endpoint and kubectl understands the new type: kubectl get crds kubectl api-resources | grep website"},{"h":"The custom resource","id":"s4","t":"Now users can create objects of that type, validated against the schema above: apiVersion: example.com/v1 kind: Website metadata: name: shop spec: image: nginx:1.27 replicas: 3 kubectl apply -f shop.yaml kubectl get websites # or: kubectl get ws At this point Kubernetes simply stores the object in etcd. Nothing runs. It is like creating an Ingress with no ingress controller: the rule exists but nobody enforces it."},{"h":"The controller and the reconcile loop","id":"s5","t":"A controller's whole job is one repeating idea, the reconcile loop: Observe the desired state (what the custom resource says). Compare it with the actual state (what really exists in the cluster). Act to close the gap: create, update or delete things. Repeat whenever something changes. For our Website, a controller might create a Deployment with 3 replicas and a Service. If someone deletes the Deployment, the controller notices and creates it again. This is the same pattern the built-in Deployment controller uses to keep Pods running (see Lesson 5). The CRD teaches the API server a new type. The controller turns custom resources into real objects. Try it: reconcile loop The custom resource asks for a number of Pods. Break things or change the request, then press Run controller to watch it fix the difference. Press a button."},{"h":"Who uses this, and who writes it?","id":"s6","t":"Many well-known tools are \"CRDs plus a controller\": Istio (VirtualService), Argo CD (Application), Prometheus Operator (Lesson 14), cert-manager and more. For a typical DevOps engineer the three steps are: Install the CRDs (usually from the project's docs, a Helm chart or an operator). Install the controller, cluster-wide or for specific namespaces. Teams then create custom resources in the namespaces that need the feature. Most DevOps engineers use CRDs but do not write them. If you do need to write a controller: Go is the most popular language, because Kubernetes itself is written in Go and its client-go library is mature. Python and Java clients also exist. Under the hood the controller registers watchers for your resource type. Events (created, updated, deleted) go into a work queue, and workers pull items off it and reconcile them. Frameworks hide most of this. controller-runtime, Kubebuilder and the Operator SDK are the common choices. NoteAn operator is a custom controller that encodes human operations knowledge (install, back up, upgrade an app such as a database). Think of it as a controller with a job title. Watch outDeleting a CRD deletes every custom resource of that type. Treat CRDs with the same care as a database schema."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"You applied a custom resource and it shows in kubectl get, but nothing happens. What is most likely missing? The CRD already exists (otherwise the apply would have failed). Without a controller, nobody acts on the stored object. What does the reconcile loop do? Observe, compare, act, repeat. It is the core of every Kubernetes controller. What is the difference between a CRD and a CR? Like the difference between the Deployment type and one specific Deployment you create."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"CRDs extend the Kubernetes API with new object types, and validate objects of those types. A custom resource is one object of such a type; by itself it is only stored data. A custom controller watches custom resources and makes reality match them via the reconcile loop. Typical flow: install CRD, install controller, create custom resources. Go with controller-runtime, Kubebuilder or Operator SDK is the usual way to build one."}],"n":12,"slug":"12-crds-custom-controllers"},{"title":"Project: ConfigMaps & Secrets","lead":"An app needs settings (a database port, a username, a password). Hard-coding them into the image is a bad idea. In this hands-on project you will store them in ConfigMaps and Secrets, feed them to a running app, and see what happens when the values change.","sections":[{"h":"Why not hard-code configuration?","id":"s1","t":"Picture a back-end app that talks to a database. It needs the database port, username, password, connection type and so on. If these are baked into the code or the container image: Changing a value means rebuilding and redeploying the image. You may not even know the values when you build the image; they might come later from a database admin. The same image cannot be used in dev, staging and production. The general rule, outside Kubernetes too, is to keep the app generic and give it settings from the outside, as environment variables or as files. Kubernetes provides two objects for this. ConfigMap Stores non-sensitive settings as key-value pairs or whole files: ports, feature flags, config files. Secret Stores sensitive values: passwords, tokens, certificates. Handled with extra care by Kubernetes. Two sources, two delivery methods."},{"h":"Step 1: create a ConfigMap","id":"s2","t":"Start with a ConfigMap holding the database port. apiVersion: v1 kind: ConfigMap metadata: name: test-cm data: db-port: \"3306\" kubectl apply -f cm.yaml kubectl get cm kubectl describe cm test-cm Prefer kubectl apply over create because you can re-run it safely after editing the file. A one-liner also works, which is handy for quick tests: kubectl create configmap test-cm --from-literal=db-port=3306"},{"h":"Step 2: use it as an environment variable","id":"s3","t":"Take a Deployment (see Lesson 5) and add an env entry to the container that reads from the ConfigMap: spec: containers: - name: app image: nginx env: - name: DB_PORT valueFrom: configMapKeyRef: name: test-cm key: db-port Apply it, then look inside a Pod: kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml kubectl get pods kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- env | grep DB # DB_PORT=3306 The app can now read DB_PORT like any environment variable (for Python, os.environ[\"DB_PORT\"]). TipIf the YAML is rejected with an error such as \"unknown field\", check the spelling against the docs. The correct field is configMapKeyRef, not configMapRef (which belongs to a different place, envFrom). Mistakes like this are normal; the API server's message tells you exactly which field it did not understand. The catch: environment variables do not update Now edit the ConfigMap and change the port to 3307, then apply it. Exec into the Pod again: DB_PORT is still 3306. A container's environment is set when it starts and cannot change while it runs. Your app would keep using the old port and fail to connect. Restarting the Pods fixes it (kubectl rollout restart deployment/<name>), but in production you want to avoid needless restarts. That is where volumes help."},{"h":"Step 3: mount it as a volume","id":"s4","t":"A ConfigMap can be mounted as a volume. Each key becomes a file, and its content is the value. Replace the env block with: spec: containers: - name: app image: nginx volumeMounts: - name: db-connection mountPath: /opt volumes: - name: db-connection configMap: name: test-cm Two things happen: volumes (at Pod level) declares where the data comes from, and volumeMounts (inside the container) says where to put it. Check the result: kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- cat /opt/db-port # 3306 Now change the value in the ConfigMap and apply it. Do not restart anything. After a short delay (typically up to a minute) the file inside the running Pod shows the new value: kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- cat /opt/db-port # 3307 Watch outThe file updates, but your app must re-read it to notice. Also, files mounted with subPath are not updated automatically. Environment variable Mounted volume App reads it as Variable File Picks up changes Only after the Pod restarts Yes, automatically after a short delay Best for Values that rarely change Values or config files that may change; whole config files"},{"h":"Step 4: Secrets","id":"s5","t":"Secrets work the same way as ConfigMaps (same env and volume patterns), but are meant for sensitive data. Create one from the command line: kubectl create secret generic test-secret --from-literal=db-password=S3cr3t Or write it in YAML. Use stringData to type plain text and let Kubernetes encode it: apiVersion: v1 kind: Secret metadata: name: test-secret type: Opaque stringData: db-password: S3cr3t Use it in a Pod exactly like the ConfigMap, swapping the key names: env: - name: DB_PASSWORD valueFrom: secretKeyRef: name: test-secret key: db-password # or as a volume volumes: - name: creds secret: secretName: test-secret There are other Secret types too, for example kubernetes.io/tls for certificates (used by Ingress TLS) and generic/Opaque for plain key-value data. The base64 caveat Look at a Secret with kubectl get secret test-secret -o yaml and the value looks scrambled. It is only base64 encoded, which is a way to write bytes as text, not encryption. Anyone can decode it: echo 'UzNjcjN0' | base64 --decode # S3cr3t Try it: base64 is not secret Type a password to see its base64 form, or paste a base64 value from a Secret to decode it. Plain text Base64 (what you see in a Secret) Edit either box."},{"h":"So how are Secrets any safer?","id":"s6","t":"On their own, Secrets are not very secret. They are safer only if you add protections: Encrypt at rest. By default the API server stores Secrets in etcd with only base64. You can enable encryption of etcd data (or use an external KMS), so a stolen etcd copy is unreadable. Managed clusters often offer this as a setting. Lock down access with RBAC. Anyone who can get secrets can read them. Give that permission to as few people as possible (least privilege). See Lesson 11: RBAC. Use external tools such as HashiCorp Vault or Sealed Secrets if you need stronger protection, and never commit real Secret YAML to Git. That is the interview answer to \"ConfigMap vs Secret\": same mechanics, but Secrets are for sensitive data and can be encrypted and access-controlled separately. More hardening is in Lesson 23. PracticeRepeat this project with a Secret holding db-password: first consume it as an env variable with secretKeyRef, then as a volume, and compare how each reacts when you change the value."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"You change a ConfigMap that a Pod reads through an environment variable. What happens to the running Pod? Environment variables are set at container start. Mounted volumes, in contrast, are refreshed. Is base64 in a Secret a good form of protection? Base64 is trivially reversible. Real protection comes from encrypting etcd and restricting who can read Secrets. Where do volumes and volumeMounts go in a Pod spec? The Pod declares the volume; each container says where to mount it."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Keep configuration out of images: ConfigMap for non-sensitive data, Secret for sensitive data. Consume either as environment variables (configMapKeyRef / secretKeyRef) or as mounted files. Env variables are fixed at start; mounted volumes refresh automatically after a short delay (not with subPath). Secret values are base64-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone can decode them. Protect Secrets with encryption at rest, tight RBAC and never storing them in Git."}],"n":13,"slug":"13-configmaps-secrets-project"},{"title":"Monitoring: Prometheus & Grafana","lead":"When something goes wrong in a cluster, you want to find out before your users do. Prometheus collects numbers (metrics) from the cluster, Grafana turns them into dashboards, and Alertmanager tells you when something is off.","sections":[{"h":"Why monitor?","id":"s1","t":"With one small cluster and one engineer, you might just look around by hand. Reality quickly gets bigger: Several teams share a cluster, and one reports \"my service was unreachable for a minute\". How do you find out what happened? Dev, staging and production each have a cluster, and the count keeps growing. Questions like \"do all Deployments have their expected replicas?\" or \"is the API server slow?\" need data, not guesses. A monitoring platform records numbers over time so you can look at history, spot trends and get alerted. Prometheus is the standard open-source choice for Kubernetes (it started at SoundCloud and is now a community project)."},{"h":"How Prometheus works","id":"s2","t":"Metrics are numbers with a name and labels, such as CPU used or HTTP requests served, sampled over time. Prometheus pulls (called \"scraping\") metrics at regular intervals from HTTP endpoints, usually /metrics. It stores them in a time-series database: each value is saved with its timestamp, on local disk. You query the data with PromQL, either in Prometheus' own web UI or via its HTTP API (so tools like curl or Grafana can use it). Rules can raise alerts, which Prometheus sends to Alertmanager. That component groups them and notifies Slack, email or similar. Prometheus pulls metrics, keeps history, raises alerts. Grafana asks Prometheus for data to draw. Where do the metrics come from? The Kubernetes API server exposes metrics about itself and the cluster out of the box (see Lesson 2). kube-state-metrics is a small add-on that turns object state into metrics: how many replicas does each Deployment want versus have, which Pods are pending, and so on. The API server alone does not give you that. Exporters are helper programs that expose metrics for things that do not speak Prometheus themselves (for example node-exporter for machine CPU and memory). Your own apps can expose a /metrics endpoint using a Prometheus client library. You then tell Prometheus to scrape it by adding a scrape target in its configuration."},{"h":"Grafana: making metrics readable","id":"s3","t":"PromQL results are raw numbers or JSON, which is hard for a manager (or you at 3 a.m.) to read. Grafana connects to Prometheus as a data source and draws charts, gauges and tables. It supports many other data sources too. Dashboards are built from PromQL queries, but you do not have to write them yourself: Grafana.com offers ready-made dashboards you can import by ID."},{"h":"Hands-on: install both with Helm","id":"s4","t":"You need any cluster (a local one such as minikube or kind works; see Lesson 26) and Helm. This lesson only uses Helm commands; how Helm works is explained in Lesson 27. Install Prometheus. Always run helm repo update first so you get a current chart version. helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts helm repo update helm install prometheus prometheus-community/prometheus kubectl get pods The chart installs the Prometheus server, Alertmanager, kube-state-metrics and node exporters. Read the notes Helm prints at the end; they show how to reach the server. Check the Services. They are all type ClusterIP (internal only): kubectl get svc Open the Prometheus UI. For a quick lab, expose it as a NodePort (Services); or just port-forward: kubectl expose service prometheus-server --type=NodePort \\ --target-port=9090 --name=prometheus-server-ext minikube service prometheus-server-ext # or use node IP + NodePort # alternative, works anywhere kubectl port-forward svc/prometheus-server 9090:80 Install Grafana and get its generated admin password: helm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts helm repo update helm install grafana grafana/grafana kubectl get secret grafana -o jsonpath=\"{.data.admin-password}\" | base64 --decode; echo The username is admin. (Yes, the password sits in a Secret; see Lesson 13.) Reach Grafana the same way: kubectl port-forward svc/grafana 3000:80 Add the data source. In Grafana go to Connections, Data sources, Add data source, Prometheus. For the URL use the in-cluster address http://prometheus-server (same namespace) and press Save & test. Import a dashboard. Go to Dashboards, New, Import, enter a dashboard ID from Grafana.com (the demo used 3662, a general Prometheus overview), pick your Prometheus data source and import. Hover over any panel to see the query behind it, which is a great way to learn PromQL. Watch outExposing Prometheus and Grafana with NodePort is fine for a lab only. In real environments put them behind an Ingress with authentication. TipMany teams install the whole stack with the kube-prometheus-stack chart, which uses an operator (CRDs plus a controller, see Lesson 12) to manage Prometheus, Alertmanager and Grafana together. Operators can also handle upgrades for you."},{"h":"A taste of PromQL","id":"s5","t":"# is each target up? (1 = yes, 0 = no) up # Deployments that don't have all their replicas kube_deployment_status_replicas_available < kube_deployment_spec_replicas # per-second HTTP request rate over 5 minutes rate(http_requests_total[5m]) The first two work with the setup above; the third needs an app that exports http_requests_total."},{"h":"Alerts","id":"s6","t":"Dashboards need someone watching. An alert rule lets Prometheus watch for you: \"if this expression stays true for N minutes, fire an alert\". The for part avoids waking people up over a one-second spike. Alertmanager then sends the notification. groups: - name: example rules: - alert: HighMemory expr: container_memory_working_set_bytes > 500 * 1024 * 1024 for: 5m labels: severity: warning annotations: summary: \"Container is using more than 500 MB\" Try it: scrape and alert Each press of Scrape adds a new sample (a random CPU %). The alert fires only if the last N samples are all above the threshold, just like for: in a rule. Threshold: 70% Must hold for: 3 scrapes Press Scrape."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"How does Prometheus get its data? Prometheus scrapes /metrics endpoints and stores samples in its time-series database. What is the role of Grafana? Prometheus stores and queries; Alertmanager notifies; Grafana draws dashboards. You want to know whether Deployments have their desired replica count. Which component supplies that data? kube-state-metrics converts the state of Kubernetes objects into metrics."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Prometheus scrapes metrics, stores them as time series and answers PromQL queries. Metrics come from the API server, kube-state-metrics, exporters and your own apps' /metrics endpoints. Alertmanager receives alerts from Prometheus and notifies Slack, email and others. Grafana uses Prometheus as a data source; import ready-made dashboards by ID to start fast. Helm makes installing the stack quick; use Ingress and authentication outside of labs."}],"n":14,"slug":"14-monitoring-prometheus-grafana"},{"title":"Real Production Challenges","lead":"Three problems almost every DevOps engineer meets on live clusters: teams fighting over shared resources, Pods killed for running out of memory, and cluster upgrades. Knowing how to explain them also makes you sound very credible in interviews.","sections":[{"h":"Challenge 1: sharing one cluster between many teams","id":"s1","t":"In production, teams rarely get a cluster each. Many microservices and project teams deploy to the same cluster, so a DevOps engineer must decide how to split its CPU and memory fairly. The obvious first step is one namespace per team (for example web, payments, shipping). But namespaces by themselves are only labels and boundaries for names and permissions. They do not limit resource use. The problemSuppose the cluster has 100 CPU and 100 GB RAM. A service in the payments namespace has a memory leak and starts taking 32 GB instead of 2 GB. Everybody else now has less. Services in other namespaces get starved, crash, and show up as CrashLoopBackOff with OOMKilled. One team's bug becomes everyone's outage. The term for how far a failure spreads is its blast radius. Your goal is to shrink it, in two steps. Step 1: ResourceQuota (a cap per namespace) A ResourceQuota sets a total limit for everything in a namespace. All its Pods together cannot exceed it, whether that is 5 services or 100. apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata: name: payments-quota namespace: payments spec: hard: requests.cpu: \"10\" requests.memory: 10Gi limits.cpu: \"15\" limits.memory: 15Gi pods: \"50\" How do you pick the numbers? Ask each team to do performance benchmarking of their services and tell you what they really need. You can challenge unusual numbers in a meeting, but once the data is sound, you allocate it. If the quotas add up to more than the cluster has, your job is to add nodes. The blast radius is now one namespace instead of the whole cluster. But inside that namespace, a leaking Pod can still starve its neighbours. Step 2: resource requests and limits (a cap per container) Set them on each container. A request is the minimum the scheduler reserves; a limit is the maximum the container may use. containers: - name: payment-api image: example/payment-api:1.4 resources: requests: cpu: \"500m\" memory: 512Mi limits: cpu: \"1\" memory: 1Gi If a container goes over its memory limit, it is killed (OOMKilled). Only that one Pod suffers. If it goes over its CPU limit, it is slowed down (throttled), not killed. TipWhen a ResourceQuota covers CPU or memory, every new Pod in that namespace must specify requests and limits, or it is rejected. A LimitRange can set defaults so teams do not forget. Each layer of control shrinks the area an unhealthy app can damage. Try it: blast radius The payments namespace has a Pod that leaks memory. Toggle the controls and press Leak memory to see who gets hurt. ResourceQuota on namespaces Memory limits on Pods Press Leak memory."},{"h":"Challenge 2: a Pod keeps getting OOMKilled","id":"s2","t":"You have set the quota and limits, and still one Pod goes into CrashLoopBackOff. Remember that CrashLoopBackOff is only a status. The real reason is shown by describe: kubectl get pods -n payments kubectl describe pod <pod> -n payments # Last State: Terminated # Reason: OOMKilled # Exit Code: 137 The general debug flow is in Lesson 20: CrashLoopBackOff. Here is the production angle: Rule out noise. Without quotas and limits, an OOMKill might be caused by another team's greedy Pod. With them in place, the guilty Pod identifies itself. Check the limit is realistic against the team's benchmarking data. A limit that is too tight kills a healthy app. Raise it if the data supports it. If it still dies with a sensible limit, the app is leaking. As a DevOps engineer, collect evidence for the developers: a thread dump and heap dump for a Java app (for example with kill -3 or jmap/jstack; other languages have their own profilers), plus metrics from Prometheus and Grafana showing memory over time. Developers analyze the dumps, find the leak, and ship a new version. You deploy it. Interview angle\"How do you handle OOMKilled?\" Describe the order: confirm via describe that it is OOM, check quotas and limits, review memory usage graphs, take dumps, hand them to developers, redeploy the fix. Say that CrashLoopBackOff is a symptom, OOMKilled is the cause."},{"h":"Challenge 3: upgrades","id":"s3","t":"Kubernetes releases often, so every cluster must eventually move from, say, 1.29 to 1.30. It is risky, since the control plane and every node are touched. Teams that do it well write a manual (a runbook) with the exact steps and rehearse it. A good manual contains: Backups first: etcd snapshot and a copy of your manifests. Read the release notes. Engineers often skip this. Removed or deprecated APIs and behaviour changes can break your workloads or even the cluster. Control plane first (etcd, API server, scheduler, controller manager), in the documented order. Then the nodes, one at a time: drain a node (Pods move elsewhere), make it unschedulable (cordon), upgrade kubelet and packages, rejoin it, make it schedulable again, and move on to the next node. kubectl drain node-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data # ...upgrade kubelet and packages on node-1... kubectl uncordon node-1 The full procedure, version skew rules and rollback planning are covered in Lesson 16: Upgrading a Cluster in Production. For managing many clusters, see Lesson 3."},{"h":"How to tell these stories","id":"s4","t":"A reliable way to answer \"what challenges have you faced?\": state the situation, what went wrong, what you changed, and the result. For example: \"A shared cluster was hit by one leaking Pod. I introduced namespace quotas and Pod limits, so the problem now stays inside a single Pod and we can see exactly which one.\""},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"Namespaces alone do not stop one team from using all the memory. What does? ResourceQuota caps the total CPU, memory and object counts a namespace may use. A container exceeds its memory limit. What happens? Memory cannot be throttled, so the container is killed. CPU overuse only slows the container down. During a node upgrade, what do you do before upgrading kubelet on that node? Drain (which also cordons) moves workloads away safely before the node is touched."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"Namespaces separate teams but need a ResourceQuota to cap what each can consume. Set requests and limits on containers so a leaky app only hurts itself. Think \"blast radius\". CrashLoopBackOff is a status; check describe for the real cause such as OOMKilled. For persistent OOMs, share thread/heap dumps and metrics with developers. Upgrades need backups, release-note review, control plane first, then drain-and-upgrade each node."}],"n":15,"slug":"15-production-challenges"},{"title":"Upgrading a Cluster in Production","lead":"Kubernetes ships a new version about every three months, and only the latest three are supported. Upgrading a live cluster without downtime is a routine DevOps job and a favourite interview topic. You will learn the checks to do first, the order of the upgrade, and how to prove it worked.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"New to the pieces of a cluster? The control plane and worker nodes are explained in Lesson 2: Architecture. Managing many clusters is covered in Lesson 3."},{"h":"Why you must upgrade","id":"s1","t":"Kubernetes releases a new minor version (1.30, 1.31, 1.32 ...) roughly every three months. Only the latest three minor versions get security fixes. Cloud providers sometimes extend support a little, but plan on the latest three. A company usually runs several clusters (dev, QA/staging, pre-production, production), so you will repeat this often. The examples here use AWS EKS, a managed cluster. The steps are almost the same on AKS, GKE and self-managed clusters. Watch out: no way backA cluster upgrade is irreversible. If you go from 1.30 to 1.31 and something breaks, you cannot downgrade the cluster. Your only way back is a fresh cluster. That is why almost all of the work happens before you press the button."},{"h":"Part 1: Prerequisites (the real work)","id":"s2","t":"In production, the prep takes days. The upgrade command itself takes minutes. Optional: cordon the nodes. \"Cordon\" means mark a node unschedulable so no new Pods land on it. Teams that want extra safety announce a 1-2 hour window with no new deployments. Running apps are not touched. This is not mandatory. Read the release notes for the version you are moving to. Look for removed or deprecated APIs and changed behaviour. Example: Ingress once lived in the extensions/v1beta1 API group and moved to networking.k8s.io. A cluster that dropped the old group would stop honouring Ingress objects still written the old way. Test in lower environments first. Upgrade dev, then staging, then pre-production. Wait one to two weeks. Only then touch production. Make versions match. The nodes must be on the same version as the control plane before you upgrade the control plane. If the control plane is 1.30 and nodes are 1.29, upgrade the nodes to 1.30 first. The kubelet (the agent on each node) must be compatible with the control plane too. Check add-ons. If you run the Cluster Autoscaler, its version must be compatible with the new control plane version. Helm, Argo CD, Prometheus and friends are just controllers and Pods, so verify them in lower environments. Free IP addresses. On EKS, keep at least five free IP addresses in the subnets you gave the cluster. Upgrades temporarily need extra network addresses. Know your cluster. Know which controllers, add-ons and networking setup you have. Otherwise the release notes will not mean anything to you. Backups and a rollback planBecause you cannot downgrade, \"rollback\" means being ready to recover. Keep your manifests in Git, back up anything stateful (databases, persistent volumes, and etcd if you run it yourself), and know how you would rebuild the cluster and redeploy. On managed services the provider backs up the control plane for you, but your own data and YAML are still your job."},{"h":"Part 2: The upgrade order","id":"s3","t":"Always go in the same order, one minor version at a time (you cannot skip from 1.29 straight to 1.31): Control plane first, then the nodes, then the add-ons, then verify. Step 1: Control plane A managed control plane (EKS, AKS, GKE) means the provider keeps it highly available, secure and scaled. It does not mean it upgrades itself. You still ask for the upgrade, in the console, the cloud CLI or a tool like eksctl: eksctl upgrade cluster --name demo --region us-east-1 --version 1.31 --approve Before you run it, check the console's upgrade insights (on EKS). It flags add-ons or APIs outside the compatibility range. When it finishes, the control plane runs the new version but your nodes do not yet. Step 2: Nodes (the data plane) This is where you need to be careful. How you do it depends on how nodes were created: Managed node group Choose a rolling update. The service replaces nodes one at a time, so Pods are moved off a node before it is replaced. Best for large clusters (100+ nodes). Use the \"force\" option only when a PodDisruptionBudget blocks the drain. Self-managed nodes You do it yourself: cordon, drain, replace. For small clusters you can instead create a new node group on the new version, then delete the old one. Custom AMIs or launch templates are your job to rebuild. Draining one node by hand looks like this: kubectl cordon node-1 # stop new Pods landing here kubectl drain node-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data # replace or upgrade the node, then: kubectl uncordon node-1 # allow scheduling again drain evicts the Pods so their Deployment recreates them on other nodes. That is how the rolling behaviour of Deployments keeps your app up while nodes change. Note: PodDisruptionBudgetsA PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) says \"always keep at least N replicas running\". A drain respects it, which keeps the app safe but can make a drain wait. That is the one case where a forced update is considered. Step 3: Add-ons Cluster add-ons such as kube-proxy, the VPC CNI network plugin and CoreDNS have their own versions. Upgrade each to a version compatible with the new cluster version. On EKS this is a button or one command per add-on and usually goes smoothly."},{"h":"How do you know it worked?","id":"s4","t":"Do not check tools one by one. Run your end-to-end (functional/regression) test suite against the upgraded cluster. If real user journeys still pass, the upgrade is healthy. Also check that all nodes show Ready and the new version: kubectl get nodes kubectl version kubectl get pods -A Try it: upgrade pre-flight check Pick your versions and see whether you are ready to upgrade the control plane. Control plane now Nodes now Target version Tested in lower environments for 1-2 weeks At least 5 free IPs in the subnets Press Check. Interview angle\"How do you upgrade a production cluster with zero downtime?\" Answer in this order: read the release notes, test in lower environments, make sure nodes and kubelet match the control plane, check add-on compatibility and free IPs; then upgrade control plane, rolling-update the nodes, upgrade add-ons, and run end-to-end tests. Add that upgrades cannot be reversed, so you plan recovery from backups and Git."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s5","t":"What is the correct order for a cluster upgrade? The control plane goes first, then the nodes are brought to the same version, then the add-ons. Finish by running tests. After upgrading 1.30 to 1.31 you find a serious problem. What can you do? Cluster upgrades are irreversible. This is why testing in lower environments and having backups matter so much. Your control plane is 1.30 and your nodes are 1.29. You want 1.31. What first? Nodes must match the control plane before the next upgrade, and you move one minor version at a time."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s6","t":"Only the latest three Kubernetes versions are supported, so upgrading is a regular duty. Upgrades are irreversible. Prevent problems with release notes, lower-environment tests (1-2 weeks) and backups. Nodes and kubelet must match the control plane first; autoscaler and add-ons must be compatible; keep 5 free IPs (EKS). Order: control plane, nodes, add-ons, one minor version at a time. \"Managed\" control plane means the provider runs it, not that it upgrades itself. Use cordon/drain or rolling node updates, and verify with end-to-end tests."}],"n":16,"slug":"16-cluster-upgrade"},{"title":"Project: End-to-End App on EKS","lead":"Build something you can show on a resume: create an Amazon EKS cluster, deploy the 2048 game, and expose it to the internet through an Application Load Balancer that Kubernetes creates for you. The focus is on the build steps and why each one is needed.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This project uses ideas taught elsewhere: Deployments, Services, Ingress and ingress controllers, and Helm. Here we only wire them together on AWS."},{"h":"What is EKS?","id":"s1","t":"EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) is AWS's managed Kubernetes. If you build a cluster yourself (kubeadm, kOps), you own everything: a broken etcd, an expired certificate, a slow API server. With EKS: AWS runs the control plane (API server, etcd, scheduler, controllers) across several Availability Zones, with an SLA. You never see those machines. You choose the worker nodes (the data plane): your own EC2 instances (in node groups with autoscaling), or Fargate, AWS's serverless compute for containers, where you do not manage servers at all. The architecture of control plane and nodes is in Lesson 2. Note that \"managed\" does not mean \"self-upgrading\". See Lesson 16."},{"h":"The plan","id":"s2","t":"A Pod is only reachable inside the cluster. To reach it from the internet, you need a path. In a VPC, apps live in private subnets and the load balancer lives in public subnets. Requests flow through the ALB to the Service and Pods. The controller (dashed) creates and configures the ALB from your Ingress."},{"h":"Step 0: Install the tools","id":"s3","t":"On your laptop you need kubectl (talks to the cluster), eksctl (creates and manages EKS clusters), the aws CLI (configured with aws configure) and helm. Use an IAM user with enough permissions in a practice account, and delete everything when done to avoid charges."},{"h":"Step 1: Create the cluster","id":"s4","t":"eksctl create cluster --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1 --fargate This takes 10-20 minutes. Behind the scenes eksctl creates a VPC with public and private subnets, the IAM roles, the managed control plane and a default Fargate profile. Doing the same by clicking in the console needs many more decisions, which is why teams prefer eksctl (or Terraform). Then point kubectl at the new cluster: aws eks update-kubeconfig --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1 kubectl get nodes HandyThe EKS console has a Resources tab where you can browse Pods, DaemonSets and ServiceAccounts without typing kubectl."},{"h":"Step 2: Create a Fargate profile for your namespace","id":"s5","t":"On Fargate, a Pod only runs if its namespace matches a Fargate profile. The default profile covers only default and kube-system. Our app will live in game-2048, so add a profile: eksctl create fargateprofile \\ --cluster demo-cluster --region us-east-1 \\ --name alb-sample-app --namespace game-2048 Watch outIf you forget this, your Pods sit in Pending forever. With EC2 node groups you do not need Fargate profiles."},{"h":"Step 3: Deploy the app, Service and Ingress","id":"s6","t":"One manifest holds four objects: a Namespace, a Deployment (5 replicas of the 2048 game image), a Service, and an Ingress. The key parts: apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: namespace: game-2048 name: service-2048 spec: type: NodePort selector: app.kubernetes.io/name: app-2048 # must match the Pod labels ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 80 --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: namespace: game-2048 name: ingress-2048 annotations: alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip spec: ingressClassName: alb rules: - http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: service-2048 port: number: 80 Apply it and look around: kubectl apply -f 2048_full.yaml kubectl get pods -n game-2048 kubectl get svc -n game-2048 kubectl get ingress -n game-2048 The Pods run, but the Ingress has no ADDRESS. An Ingress is only a set of rules. Nothing in the cluster is reading it yet. Selectors and Services are explained in Lesson 6; the Ingress idea in Lesson 9. scheme: internet-facing puts the ALB in public subnets with a public address. target-type: ip sends traffic straight to Pod IPs. It is required on Fargate. ingressClassName: alb tells the AWS controller that this Ingress is its job."},{"h":"Step 4: Install the AWS Load Balancer Controller","id":"s7","t":"The controller is itself a Pod. It must call AWS APIs to create load balancers, so it needs AWS permissions. EKS does this with IAM roles for service accounts (IRSA): a Kubernetes ServiceAccount is linked to an IAM role through an OIDC identity provider. Associate the IAM OIDC provider with the cluster: eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \\ --cluster demo-cluster --region us-east-1 --approve Create the IAM policy from the JSON published in the controller's documentation: curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/main/docs/install/iam_policy.json aws iam create-policy \\ --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --policy-document file://iam_policy.json Create a ServiceAccount bound to a role that carries the policy (replace <ACCOUNT_ID>): eksctl create iamserviceaccount \\ --cluster demo-cluster --region us-east-1 \\ --namespace kube-system --name aws-load-balancer-controller \\ --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \\ --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \\ --approve Install the controller with Helm, reusing that ServiceAccount (replace the VPC id; find it on the cluster's Networking tab): helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts helm repo update eks helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller \\ -n kube-system \\ --set clusterName=demo-cluster \\ --set serviceAccount.create=false \\ --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller \\ --set region=us-east-1 \\ --set vpcId=<VPC_ID> Wait until it is ready (2 of 2 replicas, one per Availability Zone): kubectl get deployment -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller"},{"h":"Step 5: Watch the magic happen","id":"s8","t":"As soon as the controller runs, it sees the Ingress and creates an ALB with listeners, target groups and security groups. In a couple of minutes: kubectl get ingress -n game-2048 # ADDRESS now shows the ALB DNS name Open that address in a browser (plain http://) once the load balancer shows Active in the EC2 console. You should see the 2048 game. Notice the pattern that real teams use: a DevOps engineer sets up the controller once, and every microservice team only writes Deployment, Service and Ingress YAML. Try it: why is my Ingress address empty? Tick what you have done, then see what a visitor gets. Fargate profile for game-2048 Deployment, Service and Ingress applied OIDC provider, IAM policy and ServiceAccount created Load Balancer Controller installed with Helm Press the button."},{"h":"Troubleshooting you may meet","id":"s9","t":"Controller Pods stuck at 0/2: run kubectl describe on the Deployment or ReplicaSet. A message like \"error looking up service account\" means the ServiceAccount was not created (often a leftover CloudFormation stack from a previous try; delete the stack and recreate). \"policy already exists\": an earlier run left it behind. Reuse or delete it. Pods Pending: missing Fargate profile for the namespace. ALB created but the page does not load: wait until its state is Active, and check you used http://. Clean upThe cluster, ALB and Fargate Pods all cost money. Delete the Ingress first (so the controller removes the ALB), then run eksctl delete cluster --name demo-cluster --region us-east-1."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"You applied an Ingress with ingressClassName: alb but ADDRESS stays empty. Most likely cause? An Ingress is just rules. A controller must watch it and create the load balancer. Why does the controller need an OIDC provider and an IAM-linked ServiceAccount? IRSA lets a Kubernetes ServiceAccount assume an IAM role, giving the controller Pod permission to call AWS APIs. What does \"EKS is a managed control plane\" mean? You still choose and look after the data plane (or use Fargate), and you still trigger upgrades."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"EKS = AWS-managed control plane; workers are EC2 node groups or serverless Fargate. eksctl create cluster builds the VPC, subnets, roles and cluster in one command. On Fargate, every namespace needs a Fargate profile or Pods stay Pending. An Ingress needs an ingress controller; on AWS that is the Load Balancer Controller, which creates an ALB. The controller gets AWS permissions via an OIDC provider, an IAM policy and an IAM-linked ServiceAccount. Set up the controller once; each app team then ships only Deployment, Service and Ingress."}],"n":17,"slug":"17-eks-end-to-end"},{"title":"Deployment Strategies","lead":"How do you ship version 2 of an app without users noticing? You will learn why simply deleting and reinstalling causes downtime, and how rolling update, canary and blue/green releases each reduce the risk, with a step-through demo to compare them.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Deployments, ReplicaSets and rolling updates are introduced in Lesson 5. Ingress is covered in Lesson 9. Here we compare the release strategies."},{"h":"Why do you need a strategy?","id":"s1","t":"Imagine a Payments service running version 1 on 4 replicas. The easy way to release version 2 is to delete v1, then install v2. If deleting takes 2 minutes and the new Pods need 5 more minutes to be ready, payments are down for several minutes. That means lost revenue and unhappy users. A deployment strategy is a plan for replacing the old version with the new one so downtime is zero or close to it, and so a bad release does little harm. Strategy Idea Built into Kubernetes? Recreate Stop all v1, then start v2 Yes (strategy.type: Recreate) Rolling update Replace Pods gradually Yes (the default) Canary Send a small share of real traffic to v2 No: needs an ingress controller or service mesh Blue/green Run v2 beside v1, then flip all traffic No: switch a Service or Ingress"},{"h":"1. Rolling update (the default)","id":"s2","t":"If you use a Deployment, you are already using this. Change the image and Kubernetes adds one new v2 Pod, waits until it is ready, removes one v1 Pod, and repeats until all Pods are v2. Two settings control the pace. Both default to 25%: maxSurge: how many extra Pods may exist above the desired count (25% of 4 = 1). maxUnavailable: how many Pods may be missing at once (25% of 4 = 1). apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: payments spec: replicas: 4 strategy: type: RollingUpdate rollingUpdate: maxSurge: 25% maxUnavailable: 25% selector: matchLabels: app: payments template: metadata: labels: app: payments spec: containers: - name: payments image: myrepo/payments:v1 readinessProbe: httpGet: path: /health port: 8080 Trigger a release and watch it: kubectl set image deployment/payments payments=myrepo/payments:v2 kubectl get pods -w kubectl rollout status deployment/payments kubectl rollout undo deployment/payments # go back if needed Always add a readiness probeWithout a readiness probe, Kubernetes treats a Pod as ready as soon as its container starts. It may then remove an old Pod even though the new one cannot serve traffic yet. What if the new version is broken? Say you set a non-existent image. The new Pod fails (see ImagePullBackOff) but the other three v1 Pods keep serving, and the Service only sends traffic to healthy Pods. Users are not hurt. Limits: it is \"almost\" zero downtime, not guaranteed, because a new version may need time to connect to databases or other services. You also have no control over speed or who sees v2: within minutes everyone does."},{"h":"2. Canary","id":"s3","t":"Why do other strategies exist if rolling update already avoids downtime? Because of a testing gap. v2 is tested in dev, QA and pre-production, but those have less data and smaller clusters than production. Some bugs only show up in production. Think of it like…Miners once carried a canary into the mine: if the bird fell ill, they knew to retreat before people were hurt. A canary release sends a few real users to the new version first. You run v1 and v2 together, and tell the load balancer to send, say, 10% of traffic to v2 and 90% to v1. If v2 misbehaves, only a few users are affected and you set the weight back to 0. If it looks good, raise it to 30%, 50%, then 100%. You can hold at 10% for days while the QE team runs tests. With the NGINX ingress controller, you create two of everything (Deployment, Service, Ingress), and mark the second Ingress as a canary with annotations: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: echo-canary annotations: nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary: \"true\" nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary-weight: \"10\" spec: ingressClassName: nginx rules: - host: echo.prod.mydomain.com http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: echo-canary # the v2 Service port: number: 80 The main Ingress (no canary annotation) points to the v1 Service with the same host. Test by sending 10 requests: about 9 hit v1 and 1 hits v2. Then change the weight with kubectl edit ingress echo-canary (the controller reconfigures itself in seconds). The v2 side needs fewer replicas, since it gets less traffic. Cost: more YAML and moving parts. Use it when even a small outage or bad experience is costly."},{"h":"3. Blue/green","id":"s4","t":"Keep the current version (blue) untouched and deploy a complete, identical copy of the new version (green) next to it, with its own Service. Test green privately. Then, in one step, change the Ingress (or Service selector) from the blue Service to the green Service. All traffic moves at once. If something is wrong, point it back to blue: an instant rollback. # in the Ingress backend, change: service: name: payments-blue # before # to: service: name: payments-green # after: all traffic flips Safest but most expensive: you pay for double the Pods (CPU and memory) for as long as both exist. For 100 services that is a lot, so few companies use it everywhere. Banks and financial services, where a quick rollback matters more than cost, often do."},{"h":"Compare them side by side","id":"s5","t":"Rolling Canary Blue/green Downtime Almost none Almost none None Who sees v2 Everyone, quickly A chosen percentage Nobody, then everybody Rollback Roll back again (minutes) Set weight to 0 (instant) Flip back (instant) Extra cost Small Small Double the Pods Complexity Lowest Medium Medium Try it: step through a release Choose a strategy and press Next step to see which Pods exist and where traffic goes. v1 oldv2 newoff = no Pod Interview angle\"What deployment strategies do you know?\" Rolling update is the default; canary tests v2 on a slice of real production traffic with easy rollback; blue/green is the safest but doubles resource cost. Mention maxSurge/maxUnavailable and readiness probes, and that canary and blue/green need an ingress controller (or similar) because Kubernetes does not do them natively."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"What are the default maxSurge and maxUnavailable of a rolling update? Both default to 25%. With 4 replicas, that means one extra Pod and at most one unavailable Pod at a time. Which strategy lets you test v2 on production with only about 10% of users? Canary routes a weighted share of traffic to the new version, so a bug affects only a few users. Why is blue/green used by fewer companies than canary? It is very safe with instant rollback, but running two complete environments is expensive."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"Deleting and reinstalling causes downtime; a strategy replaces versions safely. Rolling update is Kubernetes' default: gradual replacement controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable; add readiness probes. Canary sends a small, adjustable share of real traffic to v2, so production testing is low-risk. Blue/green runs two full copies and flips traffic in one step: safest, but costly. Canary and blue/green are done with an ingress controller (annotations or a changed backend), not by Kubernetes alone."}],"n":18,"slug":"18-deployment-strategies"},{"title":"Troubleshooting: ImagePullBackOff","lead":"One of the first errors every Kubernetes beginner meets: the Pod never starts because its container image cannot be downloaded. You will learn what the name means, the three common causes, how to tell them apart with kubectl describe, and how to fix each.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Pods and their YAML are explained in Lesson 4. Secrets (used below for registry logins) are covered in Lesson 13."},{"h":"What the name means","id":"s1","t":"Before a Pod can run, the kubelet (the agent on the node) must pull the container image from a registry such as Docker Hub, Amazon ECR or Azure ACR. If the pull fails you see two statuses, one after the other: ErrImagePull: the first failed attempt. ImagePullBackOff: Kubernetes is now waiting before trying again. The wait grows each time (roughly 5s, 10s, 20s, 40s ... up to a cap of 5 minutes). This growing delay is the \"back-off\". The wait exists because the failure may be temporary, like a network blip. Kubernetes keeps trying, but politely. It affects any Pod, whether created directly or by a Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet or ReplicaSet. Each failed pull moves the Pod around this loop, with a longer pause each time."},{"h":"Cause 1: wrong image name or tag","id":"s2","t":"The most common cause is a typo, or an image or tag that does not exist (or was deleted). An image reference has the shape registry/repository:tag. With no registry, Docker Hub is assumed. containers: - name: nginx image: nginy:1.14.2 # typo: there is no \"nginy\" repository Same problem for myapp:1.1.1 when that tag was removed from the registry. For a registry other than Docker Hub you must include its address, for example 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/myapp:v1. Fix: correct the image in the manifest and re-apply. A Deployment creates fresh Pods: kubectl apply -f nginx-deploy.yaml # or fix it directly: kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.14.2"},{"h":"Cause 2: private image, no credentials","id":"s3","t":"The image exists but the repository is private. Without a login, the registry refuses the pull (\"pull access denied\" or \"authorization failed\"). Kubernetes needs the credentials, stored in a Secret of type docker-registry and referenced from the Pod as an imagePullSecret. Create the Secret (the name regcred is your choice): kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \\ --docker-server=https://index.docker.io/v1/ \\ --docker-username=<your-username> \\ --docker-password=<your-password-or-token> \\ --docker-email=<your-email> Check it with kubectl get secrets. The type shows as kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson. Reference it in the Pod spec, in the same namespace: spec: imagePullSecrets: - name: regcred containers: - name: nginx image: myuser/nginx-image-demo:v1 Apply and watch: kubectl apply -f nginx-deploy.yaml then kubectl get pods -w. Watch outTwo classic slips even after adding the Secret: using the image name without your username prefix (nginx-image-demo instead of myuser/nginx-image-demo), and a Secret in a different namespace from the Pod. Also a typo in the username or password makes the Secret useless. Other registries: the same command works. Only the values change. For Amazon ECR: kubectl create secret docker-registry ecr-cred \\ --docker-server=<ACCOUNT_ID>.dkr.ecr.<REGION>.amazonaws.com \\ --docker-username=AWS \\ --docker-password=\"$(aws ecr get-login-password --region <REGION>)\" For Azure ACR or others, look up the registry's docs for the server name and credentials. ECR tokens expire after 12 hours, so teams usually refresh them automatically, or on EKS give the nodes an IAM role that can read ECR."},{"h":"Cause 3: network or registry problems","id":"s4","t":"The name is right and the credentials are fine, but the node cannot reach the registry: no internet or NAT route from a private subnet, a firewall or proxy, DNS failure, or the registry itself is down or rate-limiting you. These often clear up on their own, which is why Kubernetes keeps retrying. If not, test from the node itself (for example curl the registry or try docker pull/crictl pull) and fix routing or DNS."},{"h":"The debug flow","id":"s5","t":"kubectl get pods: confirm the status is ErrImagePull or ImagePullBackOff. kubectl describe pod <pod-name>: scroll to the Events section at the bottom. This is where the real reason is written. Read the message and match it to a cause (table and demo below). Fix, re-apply, and watch with kubectl get pods -w (the -w flag keeps updating the list). kubectl describe pod nginx-deployment-7c8f5d-abcde ... Events: Warning Failed Failed to pull image \"nginy:1.14.2\": ... repository does not exist or may require authorization Warning Failed Error: ErrImagePull Normal BackOff Back-off pulling image \"nginy:1.14.2\" Warning Failed Error: ImagePullBackOff You can also list cluster events directly: kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp. What the event says Likely cause Fix repository does not exist / manifest unknown / not found Wrong name or tag Correct the image reference pull access denied / unauthorized / authorization failed Private image, missing or wrong credentials Create docker-registry Secret, add imagePullSecrets i/o timeout / no such host / connection refused Network, DNS or registry down Fix routing, DNS, proxy; retry later Try it: read the event, find the cause Pick an event message from kubectl describe pod. Choose a message and press Diagnose. Interview angle\"A Pod is in ImagePullBackOff. What do you do?\" Run kubectl describe pod and read the Events. Then check three things: is the image name and tag right, is it a private registry that needs imagePullSecrets, and can the node reach the registry. Explain that \"back-off\" means Kubernetes retries with growing delays."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s6","t":"What does the \"BackOff\" in ImagePullBackOff mean? After each failure the delay grows (up to about 5 minutes) before the next attempt. The event says \"pull access denied\" for an image that definitely exists. What is the likely fix? A private repository needs credentials. Store them in a Secret and reference it from the Pod. Where do you find the actual reason for the failed pull? The container never started, so there are no logs. The kubelet's pull errors appear in Events."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s7","t":"ErrImagePull is the first failure; ImagePullBackOff is the waiting state with growing retry delays. Three causes: wrong image name or tag, private registry without credentials, network or registry problems. Always start with kubectl describe pod and read the Events at the bottom. Private images need a docker-registry Secret and imagePullSecrets in the same namespace. The same approach works for ECR, ACR and others, only the server and credentials differ."}],"n":19,"slug":"19-imagepullbackoff"},{"title":"Troubleshooting: CrashLoopBackOff","lead":"Your Pod starts, crashes, restarts, crashes again. Kubernetes shows CrashLoopBackOff. It is a symptom, not a single bug, so you will learn the loop itself, the most common causes (bad command, failing probe, out of memory, app errors) and a debug flow built on logs --previous and describe.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Pods are covered in Lesson 4; the Deployment that keeps restarting them in Lesson 5. A different start-up failure, a bad image, is in Lesson 19: ImagePullBackOff."},{"h":"What is happening","id":"s1","t":"When you apply a Deployment, the scheduler picks a node and the node's kubelet runs the Pod. Its normal life: ContainerCreating, then Running. But the application inside the container can exit or be killed at any moment: right away, after 5 seconds or after a day. By default a Pod's restartPolicy is Always, so the kubelet restarts the container each time. If it keeps crashing, Kubernetes inserts a growing pause between restarts: about 10s, 20s, 40s ... capped at 5 minutes. A crash repeating in a loop with a back-off delay is CrashLoopBackOff. The restart loop. Status flips between Error, CrashLoopBackOff and Running."},{"h":"Common causes","id":"s2","t":"1. Wrong command or arguments The container's start command is wrong, for example python3 app1.py when the file is app.py. It fails instantly every time. 2. Failing liveness probe The kubelet checks health; if the check keeps failing it kills and restarts the container. 3. Out of memory (OOMKilled) The container uses more memory than its limit, so the system kills it. 4. App or config errors Exceptions, a missing environment variable, a missing ConfigMap/Secret, a database that is unreachable, or a volume that does not exist. Cause 1: wrong command # Dockerfile CMD [\"python3\", \"app1.py\"] # file is really app.py Status sequence: ContainerCreating, Error, CrashLoopBackOff, Error again ... Fix the Dockerfile (or the command/args in the Pod spec), build and push a new tag (say v2), update the image in the Deployment and apply. The old Pod terminates and the new one runs. Cause 2: liveness probe fails A liveness probe is a health check the kubelet runs: it works like a load balancer's health check. If it fails repeatedly, the kubelet restarts the container, because a hung app can't answer users. (A readiness probe is different: it only decides whether the Pod receives traffic.) Real probes usually call an HTTP endpoint such as /health; a 200 means healthy and a 500 or 404 means unhealthy. livenessProbe: exec: command: [\"cat\", \"/tmp/healthy\"] # file never exists initialDelaySeconds: 5 periodSeconds: 5 failureThreshold: 3 The container runs, the probe fails, it restarts, repeats. In real life the culprits are a wrong path or port, a probe that starts checking before the app has warmed up (raise initialDelaySeconds, or use a startupProbe), or an app that really is unhealthy. Cause 3: out of memory In a shared cluster, you protect neighbours with requests (what the scheduler reserves) and limits (the maximum a container may use). Per-namespace caps are done with a ResourceQuota. If an app leaks memory or the limit is too small, it is killed: resources: requests: memory: \"64Mi\" cpu: \"100m\" limits: memory: \"128Mi\" # container is killed if it goes above this cpu: \"250m\" CPU is measured in millicores: 1000m is one CPU, so 25m is 1/40 of a CPU. Going over a CPU limit only slows the app; going over a memory limit kills it. Fix: raise the limit if it is simply too low, or report the leak to the developers (with the logs) if the app should not need that much. Cause 4: errors inside the app or its config Exceptions at start-up, a required environment variable that is missing or wrong, a Secret or ConfigMap that does not exist, a database that is not reachable. The app logs usually say exactly what happened."},{"h":"The debug flow","id":"s3","t":"kubectl get pods: see CrashLoopBackOff and the RESTARTS count. kubectl logs <pod> --previous: the logs of the crashed run. Plain kubectl logs may show the new, empty container. Add -c <container> if the Pod has more than one. kubectl describe pod <pod>: look at Last State (Reason and Exit Code) and the Events (for example \"Liveness probe failed\"). Match the evidence to a cause, fix the manifest or image, and apply. kubectl get pods kubectl logs my-app-5d9c7-xk2lp --previous kubectl describe pod my-app-5d9c7-xk2lp # in describe, look for: Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled Exit Code: 137 Clue Points to Reason OOMKilled, exit code 137 Memory limit too low or a memory leak Events: \"Liveness probe failed\" Probe path, port or timing wrong; or app unhealthy Exit code 1 and a stack trace in --previous logs Application error or bad configuration Exit code 127 or \"executable file not found\" or \"can't open file\" Wrong command or file name Try it: the back-off clock Each restart waits longer than the last, doubling until 5 minutes. Drag the slider to see how long Kubernetes waits before restart number N. Restart number: 1 Interview angle\"Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. How do you debug?\" Say: kubectl logs --previous for the crashed run, kubectl describe for exit code, reason and events; then name the usual suspects: wrong command, failing probe, OOMKilled from low limits, app or config errors. Explain the back-off (10s doubling to 5 minutes)."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s4","t":"A container keeps restarting. Which command shows the output of the run that crashed? --previous shows the logs of the last terminated container, which holds the real error. describe pod shows Last State Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code 137. What does it mean? Exceeding a memory limit gets the container killed. Raise the limit, or fix the leak. What is the maximum wait between restarts in the back-off? The delay starts at 10s and doubles: 20s, 40s ... and is capped at 5 minutes."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s5","t":"CrashLoopBackOff means the container crashes repeatedly and Kubernetes restarts it with growing delays (10s up to 5 min). It has many causes: wrong command, failing liveness probe, OOMKilled, app or config errors. Debug with kubectl logs --previous and kubectl describe pod (Last State, exit code, Events). Memory over the limit kills the container; CPU over the limit only slows it. Tell a liveness probe (restart me) from a readiness probe (send me traffic) when you diagnose."}],"n":20,"slug":"20-crashloopbackoff"},{"title":"Scheduling: Selectors, Affinity & Taints","lead":"By default Kubernetes picks any node with enough room for your Pod. Sometimes you need more control: \"only on ARM nodes\", \"prefer SSD\", \"keep everything off this node during an upgrade\". You will learn four tools for that and how to fix the Pending Pods they can cause.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"The scheduler is a control plane component, explained in Lesson 2: Architecture. To practise on several nodes locally, see Lesson 26: kind."},{"h":"What the scheduler does","id":"s1","t":"When you create a Pod, the kube-scheduler looks at every node, throws out the ones that cannot run it, and picks the best of the rest. The tools in this lesson change that decision. They come in two directions: The Pod chooses nodes nodeSelector and node affinity. \"I want to run on nodes that look like this.\" The node chooses Pods Taints and tolerations. \"I refuse Pods unless they have special permission.\" If you set these badly, no node qualifies and the Pod sits in Pending forever. That can mean downtime, so it is worth understanding properly."},{"h":"Step 1: labels on nodes","id":"s2","t":"Selectors and affinity match against labels, simple key/value tags. Nodes have labels too, and you add your own: kubectl get nodes --show-labels kubectl label node worker-2 disktype=ssd kubectl label node worker-2 disktype- # trailing dash removes the label"},{"h":"nodeSelector: the simple hard rule","id":"s3","t":"Put a nodeSelector in the Pod spec. The Pod may run only on nodes carrying every listed label. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: nginx spec: replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: nginx template: metadata: labels: app: nginx spec: nodeSelector: disktype: ssd containers: - name: nginx image: nginx:1.25 A real reason to use it: an app built only for ARM processors, or one that needs a node with fast disks or a GPU. Watch outIf no node has the label, the Pods stay Pending. Run kubectl describe pod <name> and read the Events at the bottom: Warning FailedScheduling default-scheduler 0/4 nodes are available: 4 node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector. Typos (a label on the node that says arm while the Pod asks for arm-worker) are the most common cause. Fix the label or the selector and the Pods start right away."},{"h":"Node affinity: hard or soft","id":"s4","t":"Node affinity does what nodeSelector does, with more flexibility. It has two modes: Mode Meaning Acts like requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution Schedule only if a node matches nodeSelector (a hard rule) preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution Try to match; if none, run anywhere A wish (a soft rule) The long names read as: the rule is applied during scheduling, and ignored during execution, meaning a Pod already running is not moved if labels change later. spec: affinity: nodeAffinity: preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution: - weight: 80 preference: matchExpressions: - key: disktype operator: In values: [\"ssd\"] For a required rule the shape is slightly different: requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution: nodeSelectorTerms: - matchExpressions: - key: kubernetes.io/arch operator: In values: [\"arm64\"] You do not need to memorise this syntax. Copy it from the docs. What matters is the idea: required = must, preferred = nice to have. Operators like In, NotIn, Exists let you write richer rules than a plain label match. Interview angle\"nodeSelector vs node affinity?\" Both attract Pods to labelled nodes. nodeSelector is only an exact, hard match. Affinity adds soft preferences and operators, so the scheduler has room to compromise."},{"h":"Taints: a node says \"keep out\"","id":"s5","t":"Everything so far is about what a Pod wants. A taint is the reverse: a mark on a node that repels Pods. The classic use is maintenance. Before upgrading a node you want no new Pods landing on it (see Lesson 16). kubectl taint nodes worker-1 key1=value1:NoSchedule kubectl taint nodes worker-1 key1=value1:NoSchedule- # remove it The format is key=value:effect. There are three effects: NoSchedule: no new Pods are placed here. Pods already running stay. PreferNoSchedule: the scheduler avoids the node but may use it as a last resort. Good for a node that is struggling with CPU or memory problems. NoExecute: no new Pods and existing Pods without a matching toleration are evicted. Use with care; running work stops. You may have noticed your control plane node never runs your apps. That is a taint: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule."},{"h":"Tolerations: a Pod gets an exception","id":"s6","t":"A toleration on a Pod says \"I am allowed on nodes with this taint\". Important or special Pods (monitoring agents, GPU jobs) use it. spec: tolerations: - key: \"key1\" operator: \"Equal\" value: \"value1\" effect: \"NoSchedule\" Watch outA toleration only permits; it does not attract. A tolerating Pod may land on the tainted node but may equally land elsewhere. To force a Pod onto a dedicated node, combine both: a taint plus toleration to keep others out, and a nodeSelector or affinity to pull the right Pod in. Think of it like…A taint is a \"Staff only\" sign on a door. A toleration is a staff badge. A nodeSelector is a note saying \"meet me in room 5\". The badge lets you in; it does not tell you which room to walk to. The scheduler filters out nodes that fail the selector or carry an untolerated taint."},{"h":"Which one when?","id":"s7","t":"Need Use Pod must run on a certain kind of node nodeSelector or required affinity Pod would like a certain node but can live without Preferred affinity Stop new Pods on a node (upgrade, backup) Taint with NoSchedule (or kubectl cordon) Node is unhealthy, avoid it if possible Taint with PreferNoSchedule Clear a node completely, right now Taint with NoExecute Special Pod must still run on a tainted node Toleration Try it: scheduler simulator A four-node cluster: the control plane (tainted), worker-1 (disktype=ssd), worker-2 (arch=arm) and worker-3 (disktype=ssd, tainted dedicated=batch:NoSchedule). Set up a Pod and see where it can go. Pod placement rule Toleration for dedicated=batch:NoSchedule Press Schedule the Pod."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Your Pod has a nodeSelector for gpu=true but no node has that label. What happens? nodeSelector is a hard rule. With no matching node the Pod waits in Pending, and kubectl describe pod shows why. A Pod has a toleration for a taint on node X. Where will it run? Tolerations permit; they do not attract. Add a nodeSelector or affinity to pin the Pod to the tainted node. Which taint effect also evicts Pods that are already running on the node? NoExecute evicts running Pods that do not tolerate the taint. NoSchedule only affects new Pods."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"nodeSelector and node affinity let a Pod pick nodes by label; taints let a node repel Pods. nodeSelector and required affinity are hard rules; preferred affinity is a soft wish. Taint effects: NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule, NoExecute (which also evicts). A toleration permits a Pod onto a tainted node but does not pull it there. Pending Pods? Run kubectl describe pod and read the FailedScheduling event."}],"n":21,"slug":"21-scheduling"},{"title":"StatefulSets & Persistent Volumes","lead":"Databases and other apps that must remember things need storage that outlives a Pod. You will learn how Kubernetes provides it (PV, PVC, StorageClass), how a StatefulSet differs from a Deployment, and how to fix the classic \"works on EKS, Pending everywhere else\" bug.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Stateless apps are handled by Deployments, covered in Lesson 5. Pods themselves are in Lesson 4."},{"h":"A real-world problem","id":"s1","t":"A developer files a ticket: \"My StatefulSet works on our AWS EKS cluster, but on AKS, GKE and minikube the Pods never start.\" You get the same YAML. Where do you look? To answer that you need the storage building blocks first."},{"h":"Why storage needs special handling","id":"s2","t":"A container's own filesystem disappears when the container is replaced. That is fine for a web server and a disaster for a database. Kubernetes separates asking for storage from providing storage using three objects: PersistentVolume (PV) A piece of real storage in the cluster: an AWS EBS disk, an Azure disk, a folder on a node. It exists independently of any Pod. PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) A Pod's request: \"I need 1 GiB, read/write by one node\". Kubernetes binds the claim to a matching PV. StorageClass A recipe for creating PVs on demand: which kind of storage and which provisioner. Names like standard or gp3 are classes. Provisioner / CSI driver The plugin that talks to the storage system and creates the PV when a PVC asks for it. Think of it like…A PVC is an order form (\"one 1 GiB disk, fast type, please\"). The StorageClass is the catalogue entry that says which supplier fulfils it. The provisioner is the supplier, and the PV is the disk that arrives. Dynamic provisioning: a claim triggers the creation of a volume."},{"h":"StatefulSet vs Deployment","id":"s3","t":"A StatefulSet runs Pods that need a stable identity. Compared to a Deployment: Stable names: Pods are called web-0, web-1, web-2, not random hash names. Replace web-1 and the new one is again web-1. Own storage per Pod: a volumeClaimTemplates section creates a separate PVC for each Pod (data-web-0, data-web-1...). Each Pod keeps its disk when it restarts. Ordered start: web-1 is only created after web-0 is Running and ready, then web-2 after web-1. Think of a database: you want the primary up before the replicas that copy from it. Stable DNS names for each Pod come from a headless Service, see Lesson 29. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: StatefulSet metadata: name: web spec: serviceName: web replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: web template: metadata: labels: app: web spec: containers: - name: nginx image: nginx:1.25 volumeMounts: - name: data mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html volumeClaimTemplates: - metadata: name: data spec: accessModes: [\"ReadWriteOnce\"] storageClassName: ebs # the cause of our bug resources: requests: storage: 1Gi"},{"h":"Debugging the ticket","id":"s4","t":"Apply the file on minikube and look: kubectl apply -f statefulset.yaml kubectl get statefulset # READY 0/3 kubectl get pods # only web-0, and it is Pending Only one Pod appears because of the ordered start. It is not a separate bug. Now ask Kubernetes why: kubectl describe pod web-0 # Warning FailedScheduling ... pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims \"Unbound\" means the Pod's claim never got a volume. Follow the chain from the diagram: kubectl get pvc # data-web-0 Pending kubectl describe pvc data-web-0 # storageclass \"ebs\" not found kubectl get storageclass # minikube has only: standard Root cause: the YAML asks for a StorageClass named ebs. EKS had a class by that name backed by AWS EBS, but minikube only has standard. No class, no provisioner, no volume. The fix Change storageClassName to a class that exists (standard on minikube). Better still, leave the field out and the cluster's default class is used, which makes the same YAML portable. Delete the old StatefulSet: kubectl delete -f statefulset.yaml. Delete the leftover PVCs too: kubectl delete pvc -l app=web (or by name). Deleting a StatefulSet does not delete its PVCs or volumes, on purpose, to protect your data. The old Pending claim would otherwise stay stuck with the wrong class. Apply again and watch kubectl get pods -w. web-0 goes Running, then web-1 appears, then web-2. Watch outBecause PVCs survive deletion, think before removing them in production. They hold the data. The \"delete PVC\" step above is safe only here because the claims never bound to anything. Try it: deploy the StatefulSet Pick a cluster and the storageClassName in the YAML, then apply. Cluster storageClassName in YAML Press kubectl apply."},{"h":"External storage and CSI drivers","id":"s5","t":"Clouds ship drivers for their own disks. What if you want storage from a vendor the cluster does not know, such as NetApp? The vendor supplies a CSI driver (Container Storage Interface), usually installed with a Helm chart or operator. Once installed, you create a StorageClass that points to it, and PVCs using that class get volumes from the vendor. Everything else in the chain stays the same."},{"h":"Storage troubleshooting checklist","id":"s6","t":"Pod Pending with \"unbound PVC\" → kubectl describe pvc. Does the named class exist? kubectl get storageclass (look for (default)). Is the provisioner or CSI driver running? kubectl get pods -A | grep -i provisioner. Old PVCs left over from a previous attempt? Check kubectl get pvc,pv."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"A 3-replica StatefulSet shows only web-0, which is Pending. Why are the other two missing? Ordered startup means web-1 is created only after web-0 is Running and ready. You delete a StatefulSet. What happens to its PersistentVolumeClaims? Kubernetes keeps PVCs (and the data) so deleting a StatefulSet does not destroy your database."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"A PVC requests storage; a StorageClass and provisioner create the PV that satisfies it. A StatefulSet gives Pods stable names, one PVC each, and ordered startup. \"Works on EKS, not elsewhere\" often means a missing StorageClass. Omit the name to use the default. Deleting a StatefulSet leaves PVCs behind; clean them up deliberately. Third-party storage needs a CSI driver installed first."}],"n":22,"slug":"22-statefulset-pv"},{"title":"Securing Kubernetes Like a Pro","lead":"A default cluster is built for convenience, not safety. This lesson is the DevSecOps checklist: seven layers of protection, from the API server at the front door to the container images you run, with the commands and YAML to apply each one.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Architecture terms (API server, etcd, kubelet) come from Lesson 2. RBAC is covered fully in Lesson 11, Secrets in Lesson 13, and monitoring in Lesson 14."},{"h":"Security in layers","id":"s1","t":"No single control makes a cluster safe. Good teams use defense in depth: if an attacker gets past one layer, the next one still stops them. Here are the layers, roughly from the outside in. Each layer is a separate lock. Monitoring (7) watches all of them and upgrades keep every layer patched."},{"h":"1. Secure the API server","id":"s2","t":"Every request, from kubectl, a CI job or an attacker, goes through the API server. It is the front door, so protect it first. Use TLS. Like the padlock in your browser for https, TLS encrypts traffic and proves the server is genuine. The API server is configured with a certificate, a private key and a client CA. On kubeadm-style clusters it is a static Pod defined in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml with flags: --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key --client-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt Certificates can be self-signed or issued by a certificate authority. Managed services (EKS, AKS, GKE) do this for you. Restrict who reaches it. Disable anonymous access (--anonymous-auth=false), limit network access to the API endpoint (firewall or private endpoint), and use RBAC for authorization. Keep an audit trail. Audit logging (--audit-policy-file, --audit-log-path) records who did what and when, so you can investigate later. TipProduction clusters use three control plane nodes for high availability. If one fails, the API server keeps answering from the others."},{"h":"2. RBAC: least privilege","id":"s3","t":"Authentication proves who you are; RBAC (role-based access control) decides what you may do. With thousands of users and many Pods using the API (CI tools, operators), give each only what it needs: a Role listing allowed verbs on resources, and a binding that attaches it to a user or service account. A tester should not be able to delete production Deployments. Details and commands are in Lesson 11. RBAC applies to all resources, not just the API server."},{"h":"3. Network policies","id":"s4","t":"By default every Pod can talk to every other Pod, even across namespaces. If a front-end Pod is compromised, it could reach your user database. A NetworkPolicy is a firewall rule for Pods. A common start is \"deny all incoming traffic in this namespace\", then allow only what is needed: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny-ingress namespace: finance spec: podSelector: {} # every Pod in the namespace policyTypes: - Ingress # no ingress rules listed = nothing allowed in apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: allow-api-to-db namespace: finance spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: db policyTypes: - Ingress ingress: - from: - podSelector: matchLabels: app: api ports: - protocol: TCP port: 5432 Watch outNetworkPolicies are enforced by the cluster's network plugin (CNI). Plugins such as Calico or Cilium enforce them; some simple setups silently ignore them. Test that traffic is really blocked."},{"h":"4. Pod security","id":"s5","t":"A container running as root is a risk: if it is hacked, the attacker has strong powers inside. Set safe defaults in the Pod spec: spec: securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true containers: - name: app image: myapp:1.4.2 securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: drop: [\"ALL\"] To enforce this across a namespace, use the built-in Pod Security Admission, which rejects Pods that break a standard (privileged, baseline, restricted): kubectl label namespace finance pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted"},{"h":"5. Secure container images","id":"s6","t":"Your cluster is only as safe as the images it runs. An image may contain an old base OS or a library with a known vulnerability (a bug attackers can exploit). Check images before they reach production, ideally in your CI pipeline: trivy image myrepo/myapp:1.4.2 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL # similar tools: Snyk, Clair, Quay's scanner Pin a specific base image version instead of latest; prefer small bases (slim, distroless). If scanning finds problems, update the base image or the vulnerable package, rebuild and rescan. Pull only from registries you trust; fail the pipeline on critical findings."},{"h":"6. Encrypt secrets in etcd","id":"s7","t":"Every Kubernetes object, including Secrets (passwords, tokens, keys), is stored in etcd. You may have locked Secrets down with RBAC, but an attacker who reaches etcd, or a backup of it, can read them all. Worse, a Secret is only base64-encoded by default, which is not encryption. Encryption at rest makes the API server encrypt data before writing it: apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1 kind: EncryptionConfiguration resources: - resources: [\"secrets\"] providers: - aescbc: keys: - name: key1 secret: <base64-encoded 32-byte key> - identity: {} # lets it still read old, unencrypted data Point the API server at it with --encryption-provider-config=/path/to/file. Existing Secrets are only encrypted when rewritten (kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -). Managed clouds usually offer this as a setting using their key service."},{"h":"7. Monitor, then upgrade","id":"s8","t":"Even with every control in place you need to notice trouble. Imagine someone opens a shell in a Pod and reads /etc/shadow. Runtime security tools such as Falco or Sysdig run as a DaemonSet (one Pod on every node), watch system calls, and alert on suspicious behaviour like a shell in a container or a root process. Combine this with the metrics tooling in Lesson 14. Finally, upgrade often. Old Kubernetes versions stop getting security fixes. The same goes for node operating systems and add-ons. The procedure is in Lesson 16. Try it: cluster hardening checklist Tick what your cluster already does to see how many layers you have covered. API server uses TLS, anonymous access off RBAC with least privilege NetworkPolicies (default deny) Pods run non-root (Pod Security Admission) Images scanned in CI Secrets encrypted in etcd Runtime monitoring and audit logs Regular upgrades 0 of 8 layers covered. Interview angle\"How do you secure a Kubernetes cluster?\" Walk the layers: API server (TLS, restricted access, audit), RBAC with least privilege, network policies, non-root pods, image scanning, encrypted etcd, runtime monitoring and regular upgrades. Mention defense in depth."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s9","t":"RBAC blocks users from reading Secrets, yet an attacker still reads them. Which gap is the likely cause? RBAC only protects the API. Encryption at rest protects the data stored in etcd. With no NetworkPolicies in place, which Pods can reach your database Pod? Kubernetes is open by default. A NetworkPolicy is how you restrict it. Where is the best place to scan container images for vulnerabilities? Catching problems before deployment is cheaper and safer. Tools such as Trivy fit into a pipeline step."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s10","t":"Everything goes through the API server: use TLS, restrict access and enable audit logs. Apply least privilege with RBAC and run Pods as non-root with Pod Security settings. Pods talk freely by default; NetworkPolicies restrict traffic. Scan images in CI and encrypt Secrets in etcd; base64 is not encryption. Watch runtime behaviour (Falco, Sysdig) and upgrade regularly."}],"n":23,"slug":"23-securing-kubernetes"},{"title":"kubectl Port-Forwarding","lead":"You deployed an app, but you cannot open it in your browser. kubectl port-forward creates a private tunnel from your machine to a Pod or Service. You will learn how it works, the three common situations, and how to fix the usual errors.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Services and NodePort are explained in Lesson 6 and Lesson 8. To run a local cluster like the one used here, see Lesson 26: kind."},{"h":"Port forwarding in plain words","id":"s1","t":"Port forwarding means \"connect a port on my machine to a port on another machine\". It is an old networking idea, not a Kubernetes invention. Classic example: a database on a server should only be reachable by company clients. To test it, you do not open the firewall to everyone; you create an SSH tunnel, so that localhost:3306 on your laptop secretly leads to port 3306 on the server. When you finish testing, you close the tunnel and nothing was ever exposed. kubectl port-forward does the same for the cluster network."},{"h":"Why you need it","id":"s2","t":"Pods and ClusterIP Services live on a private network inside the cluster. Your laptop is outside it. Running Kubernetes in Docker (kind, minikube with the Docker driver) adds one more layer: the \"node\" is a container with its own network, so even a NodePort is often not reachable from your browser. You could set up an Ingress (Lesson 9) or a LoadBalancer, but for a quick test that is too much work. Port-forward is the shortcut. Traffic to a local port travels through the API server to the Pod. No Service type or firewall change needed."},{"h":"The command","id":"s3","t":"kubectl port-forward <resource> <local-port>:<remote-port> [-n namespace] The resource can be a Pod, a Deployment or a Service (pod/name, deploy/name, svc/name). They all end up at a container in the end. For a Service, the remote port is the Service port; for a Pod, it is the container's port. The command keeps running in your terminal; press Ctrl+C to close the tunnel. Example: install Argo CD in a local kind cluster and open its web UI. kubectl create namespace argocd kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml kubectl get svc -n argocd # argocd-server is ClusterIP kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 9000:443 Now open https://localhost:9000 (accept the self-signed certificate warning). Changing the Service to NodePort would not have helped here, because the node is a container your laptop cannot reach."},{"h":"Use case 1: cluster on your laptop","id":"s4","t":"The default. kubectl and the browser are on the same machine, and the tunnel listens on 127.0.0.1 (localhost) only. That is also the safe choice, since nobody else on the network can use your tunnel."},{"h":"Use case 2: cluster on a cloud VM","id":"s5","t":"Say minikube runs on an EC2 instance or Azure VM and you want to use your laptop's browser. Run the port-forward on the VM, but tell it to listen on all network interfaces: kubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 svc/argocd-server -n argocd 9000:443 Then browse to https://<VM-public-IP>:9000. Remember to allow inbound port 9000 in the VM's security group or firewall. Without --address 0.0.0.0 the tunnel only listens on the VM's own localhost, which you cannot reach from outside. Watch outBinding to 0.0.0.0 opens the tunnel to anyone who can reach that port. Restrict the firewall to your own IP and close the tunnel when finished. Do not leave it running on a production machine."},{"h":"Use case 3: the local port is taken","id":"s6","t":"If you ask for 9000:80 and something already uses 9000, you get an error like bind: address already in use. Two options: find and stop that process, or let kubectl choose a free port by leaving the local port empty: kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd :443 # Forwarding from 127.0.0.1:62708 -> 8080 kubectl prints the port it picked. This is handy in scripts and CI pipelines where you cannot know which ports are free. Try it: build a port-forward command Resource type Name Namespace (blank = default) Local port (blank = auto-pick) Remote port Running on a cloud VM (open to the outside)"},{"h":"Troubleshooting","id":"s7","t":"Symptom Likely cause and fix address already in use Local port taken. Pick another or leave it blank. connection refused in the browser or in the kubectl output Wrong remote port: the app is not listening there. Check containerPort and the Service's port/targetPort. Works on the VM, not from your laptop Missing --address 0.0.0.0, or the firewall blocks the port. Tunnel stops by itself The Pod restarted or was replaced; a Pod-targeted tunnel dies with it. Re-run the command, or target the Deployment or Service. unable to forward ... pod not running The Pod is Pending or crashing. Fix that first with kubectl get pods and describe. https page shows a certificate warning Normal for self-signed certs on the app side; accept it for local tests. TipPort-forward to a Service still connects to one Pod behind it; it does not load-balance. It is a debugging and testing tool, not a way to serve users."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"What does kubectl port-forward svc/web 8080:80 do? The format is local:remote. It changes nothing in the cluster; it only opens a tunnel. Your cluster runs on an EC2 instance. You run port-forward there, but your laptop cannot connect. What is missing? By default the tunnel listens only on localhost of the machine running kubectl. How do you let kubectl pick a free local port? With an empty local port kubectl chooses a free one and prints it."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"kubectl port-forward is a temporary tunnel from a local port to a Pod, Deployment or Service port. Syntax: local:remote. It stays open until you press Ctrl+C. On a remote VM add --address 0.0.0.0 and open the firewall, carefully. Leave the local port empty (:80) to get a free random one. It is for testing and debugging. Use Ingress or LoadBalancer for real traffic."}],"n":24,"slug":"24-port-forwarding"},{"title":"Kustomize","lead":"The same app usually runs in dev, staging and production with small differences: replica counts, image tags, names. Kustomize lets you keep one base YAML and describe only the differences, with no copying and no templates.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Deployments and their replicas and image fields are explained in Lesson 5. Kustomize and Helm (Lesson 27) solve a similar problem in different ways."},{"h":"The problem","id":"s1","t":"You have an nginx Deployment. Dev needs 3 replicas, staging 4, production 5. With plain kubectl apply you would make folders dev/, stage/, prod/, each with a nearly identical copy of the file. Then six months later a security fix means updating the image in every copy. With 50 microservices and 3 environments that is 150 files to edit by hand, and one will be forgotten. Kustomize fixes this. It is built into kubectl (and also available as a standalone kustomize command) and is a template-free configuration tool: your YAML stays valid, ordinary Kubernetes YAML, and you layer changes on top. Think of it like…Ansible for servers lets you say \"install nginx on all VMs, but a different version on one\". Kustomize does the same for manifests: one shared definition, plus small per-environment tweaks."},{"h":"Bases and overlays","id":"s2","t":"A base is the common, original set of manifests (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap...). An overlay is a folder per environment that points at the base and says what to change. myapp/ ├── base/ │ ├── deployment.yaml │ └── kustomization.yaml └── overlays/ ├── dev/ │ └── kustomization.yaml └── stage/ └── kustomization.yaml The base is never edited per environment; each overlay describes only its differences."},{"h":"Step by step","id":"s3","t":"1. The base. base/deployment.yaml is a normal Deployment (name nginx-deployment, 1 replica, image nginx:1.14.2). Next to it, a kustomization.yaml lists which files belong to the base: # base/kustomization.yaml apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1 kind: Kustomization resources: - deployment.yaml Add a Service, ConfigMap or Ingress later by listing it under resources. 2. An overlay. It points at the base and adds transformers, built-in instructions for common changes: # overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1 kind: Kustomization resources: - ../../base namePrefix: dev- images: - name: nginx newTag: latest The resources path can also be a Git URL, so a platform team can keep shared bases in one repository while app teams keep only overlays. 3. Preview, then apply. kubectl kustomize overlays/dev # print the final YAML, change nothing kubectl create namespace dev kubectl apply -k overlays/dev -n dev # note: -k, not -f The Deployment is now called dev-nginx-deployment and uses nginx:latest, and base/deployment.yaml was never touched. This also solves a common team problem: two developers sharing a namespace each use a different prefix and no longer overwrite each other's Deployments."},{"h":"Common built-in transformers","id":"s4","t":"Field What it does namePrefix / nameSuffix Adds text to every resource name namespace Sets the namespace of all resources images Changes image name or tag replicas Sets replica count by resource name commonLabels / commonAnnotations Adds labels or annotations to everything The Kustomize documentation lists about a dozen. Check there before writing patches by hand."},{"h":"Patches: change anything","id":"s5","t":"What if no transformer fits, for example a field in a custom resource? Use patches. There are two styles; pick whichever you find more comfortable. Style 1: YAML (strategic merge) patch Write a small YAML file containing just enough of the resource to identify it, plus the field you change: # overlays/dev/replicas.yaml apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: nginx-deployment spec: replicas: 4 # overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml (add) patches: - path: replicas.yaml Style 2: JSON patch Give an operation and a path. Because it does not say which resource, you add a target: # overlays/stage/replicas.yaml - op: replace path: /spec/replicas value: 5 # overlays/stage/kustomization.yaml (add) patches: - path: replicas.yaml target: group: apps version: v1 kind: Deployment name: nginx-deployment Both do the same thing. YAML patches are easier to read; JSON patches are handy for precise edits such as an item inside a list of containers."},{"h":"Generators","id":"s6","t":"Kustomize can also create ConfigMaps and Secrets from literals or files with configMapGenerator and secretGenerator. It appends a hash to the name, so changing the content triggers a rolling restart of Pods that use it. They are useful but less commonly used than patches and transformers."},{"h":"Kustomize vs Helm","id":"s7","t":"Kustomize: no templates, plain YAML plus overlays. Simple, built into kubectl. Good for per-environment tweaks. Helm: templates plus a values file, and also a package manager with releases and rollbacks. More powerful, more to learn. See Lesson 27. Many teams even combine them. Pick Kustomize when you want the simpler approach. Try it: render an overlay The base is fixed. Choose an overlay and which transformers it uses, and see the output of kubectl kustomize. Changed lines are highlighted. namePrefix (dev- / stage- / prod-) images: tag (dev: latest, stage: 1.25, prod: 1.25.3) replicas (dev 3, stage 4, prod 5)"},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Which command applies an overlay folder? Kustomize directories are applied with -k. kubectl kustomize <dir> only prints the result. A new nginx security fix needs one image tag change for all environments. With a shared base, where do you edit? Overlays inherit from the base, so a shared change goes in one place. Overlays that set their own images tag keep theirs. Why choose Kustomize over Helm for a simple setup? Kustomize keeps manifests as normal YAML. Helm uses templates and adds package management."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"Kustomize customizes plain YAML without templates, and is built into kubectl. Put shared manifests in a base and per-environment changes in overlays. Each folder needs a kustomization.yaml; overlays reference the base under resources. Use built-in transformers (namePrefix, images, replicas) or patches (YAML or JSON style). Preview with kubectl kustomize, deploy with kubectl apply -k."}],"n":25,"slug":"25-kustomize"},{"title":"Kubernetes in Docker (kind)","lead":"kind runs a real Kubernetes cluster inside Docker containers on your laptop. You'll learn why it beats the usual local options, how to create single-node and multi-node clusters in seconds, and how to open your apps from the browser.","sections":[{"h":"Why a local cluster at all?","id":"s1","t":"To practise Kubernetes you need a cluster. Cloud clusters (EKS, AKS, GKE) work, but they cost money and take time to create. Many beginners rent a cloud virtual machine just to run Minikube, and the bill adds up. A local cluster is free, fast, and safe to break."},{"h":"The problem with the usual choice: Minikube","id":"s2","t":"Minikube is the best-known local option. It has two common modes: VM driver (VirtualBox, HyperKit): Minikube starts a whole virtual machine and puts the control plane and the worker in it (\"all-in-one\"). A VM wants at least about 2 CPUs and 4 GB of RAM. If your laptop is busy with other things, the VM may fail to start or get killed. Docker driver: Minikube uses a container instead of a VM. Better, but running several clusters or a multi-node cluster at once is clumsy."},{"h":"What kind does differently","id":"s3","t":"kind stands for Kubernetes in Docker. Each Kubernetes node is simply one Docker container. Inside that container run the kubelet, a container runtime and (on control-plane nodes) the API server, etcd and so on. If you need the pieces explained, see Lesson 2: Architecture. Single-node cluster = 1 container. 1 control plane + 2 workers = 3 containers. A multi-node cluster gets one extra container, a small load balancer in front of the control planes. One kind cluster: every Kubernetes node is a Docker container. Because containers start in seconds and share your laptop's kernel, kind is light. A nine-node cluster can be up in about 30 seconds, and you can keep several clusters side by side (as many as your laptop can handle)."},{"h":"Install and create your first cluster","id":"s4","t":"You need Docker running first, plus kubectl. Then install kind (for example brew install kind on macOS, or download the binary from the kind releases page). On Windows, run Docker Desktop and install kind there; WSL 2 also works. kind create cluster --name demo kind get clusters kubectl config current-context # kind-demo kubectl get nodes kind adds a context called kind-<name> to your kubeconfig and switches to it, so kubectl talks to the new cluster straight away. You can see the \"node\" as a container: docker ps | grep -i demo"},{"h":"Multi-node clusters with a config file","id":"s5","t":"A plain kind create cluster gives one node. For more, describe the nodes in a YAML file: kind: Cluster apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4 nodes: - role: control-plane - role: worker - role: worker kind create cluster --name multi --config kind-config.yaml kubectl get nodes Each - role: line adds one container. Add more worker lines for a bigger cluster, or more control-plane lines to practise a highly available control plane. Try it: design a kind cluster Choose how many control-plane and worker nodes you want. See the config file and the Docker containers kind would create. Control planes Workers 2"},{"h":"Running and reaching apps","id":"s6","t":"Cloud clusters hand out a public address when you create a LoadBalancer Service. A local kind cluster has no cloud to do that, so the address stays pending. The simplest native fix is kubectl port-forward (the details are in Lesson 24): kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx kubectl get pods kubectl port-forward pod/<pod-name> 8888:80 # open http://localhost:8888 For a Service of type NodePort or an Ingress on kind, you can publish a node's port to your laptop with extraPortMappings in the config. (Services themselves are covered in Lesson 6 and Lesson 8.) TipYour own image isn't on a registry? Build it locally and copy it into the cluster: kind load docker-image my-app:1.0 --name demo. Then use that tag in your Pod (and set imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent)."},{"h":"Clean up","id":"s7","t":"kind delete cluster --name demo kind delete cluster --name multi"},{"h":"kind vs Minikube","id":"s8","t":"kind Minikube Node is a… Docker container VM (or one container with the Docker driver) Multi-node Easy, one config file Possible, less smooth Many clusters at once Easy, just give each a name Profiles, heavier Needs Docker only Docker or a hypervisor"},{"h":"What local clusters are good for, and not","id":"s9","t":"Great for Learning objects, practising node affinity and taints, scaling, Pod disruption budgets, testing manifests and Helm charts, CI pipelines. Not great for Real cloud load balancers, rehearsing a production cluster upgrade, or building admission controllers and operators that need a realistic environment. Watch outkind is for learning and testing, not production. All nodes share one laptop, so there is no real high availability."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"You create a kind cluster with 1 control plane and 2 workers. What do you see in docker ps? Each Kubernetes node is a Docker container. Only multiple control planes add the extra load balancer container. Your LoadBalancer Service stays on <pending> in kind. Simplest way to open the app? There is no cloud load balancer behind a local cluster. Port-forwarding tunnels a local port to the Pod or Service."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"kind = Kubernetes in Docker: every node is a container, so clusters start in seconds and need no VM. kind create cluster --name x makes a cluster and a kind-x kubectl context. Use a kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4 config with role: control-plane / worker for multi-node clusters. Run several clusters side by side; delete with kind delete cluster --name x. Use port-forward (or extraPortMappings) to reach apps, and kind load docker-image for local images. Great for learning and testing; not for production or upgrade rehearsals."}],"n":26,"slug":"26-kind"},{"title":"Helm: The Package Manager","lead":"Helm installs, upgrades and removes whole applications on Kubernetes with one command. You'll learn charts, repositories, releases and values, then build and package a chart of your own.","sections":[{"h":"What is Helm?","id":"s1","t":"Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes. If you know Ubuntu, the idea is the same as apt: instead of hunting for install steps, you run one command and the tool does everything. Linux (apt) Kubernetes (Helm) package chart repository (archive.ubuntu.com) repository (e.g. Bitnami) apt install python3 helm install my-name repo/chart upgrade / remove package helm upgrade / helm uninstall"},{"h":"Why not just kubectl apply?","id":"s2","t":"You can install anything with kubectl apply, just like you can install anything on Linux with curl and shell scripts. The pain shows up at scale: A tool like Prometheus needs a namespace, a service account, config maps, a Deployment or StatefulSet, Services, and they must be created in the right order. Team A wants version 2.12 and team B wants 2.13. UAT needs 2 replicas, production needs 3. You'd keep copying and editing YAML files and scripts. Those scripts must be tested and kept current as the software changes. Helm bundles all the YAML into one chart, lets you change settings from outside, and tracks what it installed so it can upgrade or remove it cleanly."},{"h":"Four words to know","id":"s3","t":"Chart A package: all the Kubernetes YAML for an app, plus default settings. Repository A place that stores and serves charts. helm repo add registers one. Release One installed copy of a chart in a cluster, with a name you choose. Install the same chart twice and you get two releases. Values The settings you can change (replicas, image tag, resources) without editing the chart. Helm fills the chart's templates with your values, then applies the result."},{"h":"Install Helm","id":"s4","t":"Helm is a command-line tool on your laptop (brew install helm on macOS, or the installer for your OS from the Helm docs). Nothing is installed in the cluster; Helm 2 needed a server part called Tiller, Helm 3 does not. Like kubectl, Helm talks to whichever cluster is the current context in your kubeconfig. helm version kubectl config current-context"},{"h":"Installing third-party apps","id":"s5","t":"Two steps: add the repository once, then install a chart from it. helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami helm repo update helm search repo bitnami | grep -i nginx helm install nginx-v1 bitnami/nginx helm list kubectl get pods In helm install nginx-v1 bitnami/nginx, nginx-v1 is the release name and bitnami/nginx is repo/chart. The release name is required; it lets you install many copies and later upgrade or remove the right one. Not in Bitnami? Search the project's own docs. Most tools publish their own repo (for example, the AWS Load Balancer Controller documents its eks chart repo). Add it the same way. NoteHelm is used to install tools like Prometheus and Grafana too. How they work is in Lesson 14."},{"h":"Values: customising an install","id":"s6","t":"Every chart ships default settings in values.yaml. See what you can change, then override at install time: helm show values bitnami/nginx helm install web bitnami/nginx --set replicaCount=3 helm install web2 bitnami/nginx -f my-values.yaml --set is good for one or two values; -f file.yaml is better for a set you keep in Git (one file per environment)."},{"h":"Upgrade, history, rollback, uninstall","id":"s7","t":"helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx --set replicaCount=5 helm history web helm rollback web 1 helm uninstall web upgrade changes an existing release (new chart version or new values). Each change is a numbered revision, so rollback returns to an earlier one. uninstall takes the release name and removes everything the chart created."},{"h":"Build your own chart","id":"s8","t":"You can package your own microservices too. Say you work on a shop with a payments and a shipping service. Let Helm create the skeleton: helm create payments Three things matter in the folder: payments/ Chart.yaml # metadata: name, chart version, app version values.yaml # default settings templates/ # your YAML files, with placeholders charts/ # dependencies (ignore for now) templates/: put your Deployment, Service, ConfigMap etc. here. Replace fixed values with placeholders. values.yaml: the values those placeholders read. Chart.yaml: who owns it, chart version, app version. A trimmed templates/deployment.yaml: apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: {{ .Release.Name }}-payments spec: replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }} selector: matchLabels: app: {{ .Release.Name }}-payments template: metadata: labels: app: {{ .Release.Name }}-payments spec: containers: - name: payments image: \"{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}\" # values.yaml replicaCount: 1 image: repository: busybox tag: latest The Deployment and its fields are covered in Lesson 5. Here, the only new thing is the {{ }} placeholders. Try it: values fill the template Change the values and see the YAML Helm would produce. Think of it as \"UAT vs production\". Release name Replicas Image tag Package and publish helm package payments # creates payments-0.1.0.tgz helm package shipping helm repo index . # creates index.yaml listing both charts Upload the .tgz files and index.yaml to any web location (an artifact server like Nexus or Artifactory, or GitHub Pages). Anyone can then run helm repo add best-commerce <url> and helm install p1 best-commerce/payments, exactly like Bitnami charts."},{"h":"Helm vs Kustomize","id":"s9","t":"Both solve \"same app, different settings\". They take different approaches. Helm Kustomize Idea Templates + values, packaged as charts Plain YAML + patches/overlays Sharing apps Strong: repositories, versions Not a package format Lifecycle Releases, upgrade, rollback Just generates YAML Learning curve Template syntax Gentler Many teams use Helm to install third-party tools and Kustomize (or Helm values files) for their own apps. Interview angle\"What problem does Helm solve?\" Packaging, versioning and configuring many YAML files as one unit, with upgrade and rollback. Be ready to name chart, release, repository and values."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s10","t":"What is a Helm release? A chart is the package; a release is a named install of it. Install the same chart twice and you have two releases. You want 3 replicas in production and 2 in UAT, using the same chart. What do you change? Templates stay the same; values are what change per environment. How does Helm 3 know which cluster to install into? Like kubectl, Helm uses your kubeconfig. Helm 3 has no server component."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s11","t":"Helm is a package manager: chart = package, repository = store, release = installed copy, values = settings. Install flow: helm repo add, helm search repo, helm install NAME repo/chart. helm upgrade, helm history, helm rollback, helm uninstall manage the release lifecycle. Use helm show values to see options; override with --set or -f. Your own chart = Chart.yaml + values.yaml + templates/; publish with helm package and helm repo index. Helm packages and shares; Kustomize patches plain YAML. They can be combined."}],"n":27,"slug":"27-helm"},{"title":"Project: Moving Apps from VMs to Kubernetes","lead":"A realistic scenario: a company runs containerised microservices with Docker Compose on virtual machines, and you must move them to Kubernetes. This lesson is the plan: six levels, from a spreadsheet to a multi-region production setup.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This project reuses ideas from other lessons: Deployments, Ingress, RBAC, StatefulSets, monitoring. Here we focus on how to sequence the migration."},{"h":"The scenario","id":"s1","t":"You join a large company as a DevOps engineer. Their application is made of many microservices (think of an online shop: UI, payments, transactions, shipping, recommendations). Each service is already in a Docker image. Today they run with Docker Compose on VMs, with a load balancer in front. Nobody on the team knows Kubernetes, and you must \"move us to Kubernetes\". Watch outThe classic mistake: create a cluster, pick a random service, and deploy it. A migration takes weeks or months, so work in levels, each with a clear goal."},{"h":"What changes from Compose to Kubernetes","id":"s2","t":"In Docker Compose In Kubernetes a service with image: a Deployment (or StatefulSet for databases) ports: a Service (and an Ingress for outside traffic) environment: ConfigMap and Secret volumes: PersistentVolumeClaim healthcheck: liveness and readiness probes load balancer in front of VMs Ingress controller / cloud load balancer Six levels. Each one produces something you can show and test before moving on."},{"h":"Level 0: requirement gathering","id":"s3","t":"No cluster yet. You collect facts in a spreadsheet and share it with developers and management. List the microservices. What looks like one app to a user is many services (UI, payments, its database, its config service...). List the teams. Services are owned by teams (payments, shipping, UI). This later becomes your namespace layout. Rank by business impact: less critical, medium, important, critical. You migrate the least critical first, so mistakes are cheap. Measure current resource use. The most important step. Read CPU, memory and disk per service from Prometheus/Grafana, or from CloudWatch or your cloud billing dashboard. This sizes the cluster. Otherwise you may build a 50-CPU cluster you don't need. Estimate cost. Compare today's VM bill with what EKS, AKS or GKE would cost, using the provider's price calculator. Management cares about the balance of cost and performance. Sheet columns that work well: service, team, CPU, memory, business impact, onboarding priority, estimated cost, environment readiness (not ready / in progress / ready), timeline. TipDiscuss the sheet with developers and managers before you start. A plan only you have seen is a plan nobody will support."},{"h":"Level 1: proof of concept (about 2 to 4 weeks)","id":"s4","t":"You don't yet know whether each service even runs well on Kubernetes. Prove it on a small slice. Pick 15 to 20 services, a few from every impact group. Include both stateless apps and stateful ones (a database, a cache, a queue). Create a small cluster (for example 3 control-plane and 3 worker nodes of 8 CPU / 8 GB). Managed services run the control plane for you. Write manifests: Deployments, or StatefulSets and DaemonSets where needed, plus Services and an Ingress. For the ingress controller, the easiest start is your cloud's own (ALB on AWS). Test traffic and ask the QE team to run their tests. Fix what breaks. Pods in CrashLoopBackOff usually need correct config, or liveness and readiness probes. If you want to practise Level 1 on your own machine, a local cluster from kind works for the manifests part."},{"h":"Level 2: the dev cluster","id":"s5","t":"Now you build for real, using the numbers from level 0. Sizing Control plane: three nodes minimum (managed services handle this). Workers: at least three, with a little more CPU and memory than the total you measured. Example: you need 40 CPU and 60 GB, so use three nodes of 16 CPU / 24 GB (48 CPU / 72 GB). A cluster can scale to thousands of nodes (the official guideline is 5000 nodes, 110 Pods per node), but more nodes means more work during upgrades, which go node by node (see Lesson 16). Don't create 10 nodes when 3 do. Try it: cluster sizing helper Enter the totals from your sheet and a node count. The helper adds headroom and suggests a node size. Total CPU needed Total memory (GB) Worker nodes Headroom Namespaces, quotas and limits Create one namespace per team. That keeps teams apart and lets you grant each developer access only to their own namespace using RBAC. A UI developer has no reason to touch the payments database. Then stop one team (or one leaky Pod) from eating the whole cluster: ResourceQuota caps what a namespace may use in total. requests and limits (and optionally a LimitRange for defaults) cap each Pod. apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata: name: payments-quota namespace: payments spec: hard: requests.cpu: \"8\" requests.memory: 16Gi limits.cpu: \"12\" limits.memory: 24Gi pods: \"60\" Set each namespace's quota a little above what that team measured. Let developers and testers use the dev cluster for about a month before moving on."},{"h":"Level 3: staging (or QE)","id":"s6","t":"Dev is unstable by design because developers change it daily. When production has a problem you need a stable place to reproduce it. That is staging. There are two ways to build it: Same cluster, separate namespaces Cheaper. Namespaces like dev-payments and stage-payments. But RBAC must be strict, or developers will disturb staging too. A separate cluster Costs more but gives clean isolation. A replica of dev with more resources, closer to production. This is the better choice if the budget allows. How many environments you need depends on risk. A bank may have dev, QE, staging, UAT and pre-prod. A small product company may need only pre-prod and prod."},{"h":"Level 4: production","id":"s7","t":"Setup is similar, with two big additions. Multiple Availability Zones. An AZ is a data centre. Spread nodes over three of them (for example 1a, 1b, 1c) so losing one doesn't take you down. Pods must spread too. Nodes in three zones don't help if all replicas land in one. Use topology spread constraints: spec: topologySpreadConstraints: - maxSkew: 1 topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule labelSelector: matchLabels: app: payments (This goes in the Pod template of your Deployment. For other placement tools see Lesson 21.) In production you can be more generous with sizing or use autoscaling (Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter). Also set up observability (Prometheus and Grafana), probes, and tested requests and limits before you take real traffic."},{"h":"Level 5: scale production","id":"s8","t":"If customers are worldwide, a US cluster is reachable from Asia but with higher latency. For high availability and speed, run several clusters, one per region (US, Asia, Europe). Each has its own Ingress. In front, put a global load balancer or DNS routing (such as Route 53 latency or geo routing), which sends each user to the nearest cluster. It doesn't end here. Next come Helm charts (Lesson 27), security (Lesson 23), GitOps, service mesh and policy tools, and so on. Interview angle\"How would you migrate 100 services from VMs to Kubernetes?\" Answer in levels: gather requirements and measure usage, run a proof of concept on a mixed sample, build dev, then staging, then multi-AZ production, then multi-region. Mention namespace per team, quotas, probes, and migrating least-critical services first."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s9","t":"Which service should you migrate first? Rank services by business impact and start with the least critical, working up to critical ones. What is the difference between a ResourceQuota and Pod limits? Quotas keep a team within its share of the cluster; requests and limits keep each Pod within its share of that. You have nodes in three zones, but all replicas of one Deployment are on one node. What helps? Topology spread constraints tell the scheduler to place replicas across zones."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s10","t":"Migrate in levels: requirements, proof of concept, dev, staging, production, scale. Measure real CPU, memory and cost from your monitoring before sizing anything. Prove a mixed sample of 15 to 20 services before building environments. Namespace per team, with RBAC, ResourceQuota and Pod limits for fair sharing. Production needs multiple zones (and spread constraints); multi-region means several clusters behind DNS or a global load balancer. Share your plan with developers and management."}],"n":28,"slug":"28-vm-to-kubernetes"},{"title":"Headless Services","lead":"A normal Service hides your Pods behind one IP and spreads requests between them. Sometimes that is exactly what you don't want. A headless Service gives each Pod its own stable DNS name instead, which is what databases and other stateful apps need.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"Need a refresher on what a Service does, with selectors, endpoints and load balancing? See Lesson 6: Services. StatefulSets and volumes are covered in Lesson 22."},{"h":"Quick recap: why normal Services work so well","id":"s1","t":"Pod IPs change whenever a Pod is replaced. A normal Service (type ClusterIP) gives clients one stable virtual IP and DNS name. It finds Pods by label selector, and kube-proxy forwards each request to one of the healthy Pods, roughly round-robin. For stateless apps (web servers, APIs) any replica can answer, so this is perfect."},{"h":"The problem: stateful apps","id":"s2","t":"Imagine a backend talking to a database with two replicas, mysql-0 and mysql-1, each with its own volume. Request 1: \"add a new employee\". The Service sends it to mysql-0, which stores it on its volume. Request 2: \"list all employees\". The Service sends it to mysql-1, which never saw the new employee. The user sees missing data. The replicas are not interchangeable, so the Service's load balancing becomes a bug. Clients need to say which replica they want. Examples: \"write to the primary\", \"read from replica 1\", \"never send traffic to the backup node\"."},{"h":"What a headless Service is","id":"s3","t":"A headless Service is a Service with clusterIP: None. Kubernetes then gives it no virtual IP and does no proxying or load balancing. All it does is publish DNS records so that clients can find the Pods directly. Think of it like…A normal Service is a company's reception desk: you call one number and get whoever is free. A headless Service is the staff directory: it lists everyone's direct line, and you choose who to call. A normal Service chooses the Pod for you. A headless Service lets the client choose."},{"h":"The YAML","id":"s4","t":"The only difference from a normal Service is clusterIP: None. The selector still matches the Pods by label. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: mydb-headless spec: clusterIP: None selector: app: mysql ports: - port: 3306 name: mysql Then a StatefulSet whose serviceName points at that Service. That link is what gives each Pod its own DNS name. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: StatefulSet metadata: name: mysql-stateful spec: serviceName: mydb-headless replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: mysql template: metadata: labels: app: mysql spec: containers: - name: mysql image: mysql:8.0 env: - name: MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD value: \"demo-only-password\" # use a Secret in real life ports: - containerPort: 3306 TipCreate the headless Service first, then the StatefulSet. DNS is the whole point, so the Service should exist when the Pods start."},{"h":"The DNS names you get","id":"s5","t":"Each StatefulSet Pod gets a predictable name (mysql-stateful-0, -1, -2), and the headless Service publishes a DNS record for each: <pod-name>.<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local mysql-stateful-0.mydb-headless.default.svc.cluster.local mysql-stateful-1.mydb-headless.default.svc.cluster.local The Pod's IP can change when it is recreated, but the name keeps pointing to the right replica. Your backend can safely put mysql-stateful-0.mydb-headless... in a ConfigMap. The Service name alone (mydb-headless.default.svc.cluster.local) returns the IPs of all ready Pods, which is useful for clients that discover every member themselves, as in database clusters."},{"h":"Try it on a cluster","id":"s6","t":"kubectl apply -f headless-svc.yaml kubectl apply -f statefulset.yaml kubectl get svc # CLUSTER-IP shows None kubectl get pods -o wide # note the IP of mysql-stateful-0 kubectl run -it --rm dns-test --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- \\ nslookup mysql-stateful-0.mydb-headless.default.svc.cluster.local kubectl delete pod mysql-stateful-0 # wait for it to come back, then run the nslookup again: # same name, new IP The CLUSTER-IP column showing None confirms it is headless, and the repeated lookup shows the DNS name following the Pod through a restart. Try it: normal vs headless lookups Pick a Service type, send a few requests or do an nslookup, then restart mysql-0 to see what changes. Choose a type, then try the buttons."},{"h":"When to use which","id":"s7","t":"Normal (ClusterIP) Headless clusterIP virtual IP assigned None Proxying and load balancing Yes No DNS returns the Service IP Pod IPs (and per-Pod names with a StatefulSet) Best for Stateless apps Databases, Redis, Kafka, clustered apps A common pattern for stateful systems is to use a headless Service for peer-to-peer or primary/replica access, and a normal Service in front when load balancing reads is fine. Interview angle\"What is a headless Service and when do you use it?\" A Service with clusterIP: None: no virtual IP and no load balancing, just DNS records for individual Pods. Used with StatefulSets when clients must reach a specific replica."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s8","t":"Which field makes a Service headless? Setting clusterIP: None tells Kubernetes not to allocate a virtual IP or do proxying. Why is a normal Service a poor fit for a two-replica database where each replica has its own storage? Round-robin treats replicas as interchangeable, but database replicas hold different data and roles. What happens to mysql-stateful-0.mydb-headless... when the Pod is deleted and recreated with a new IP? StatefulSet Pods keep their identity, so the DNS name keeps pointing to the replacement Pod."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s9","t":"Normal Services proxy and load balance, which suits stateless apps but not replicas with different data. A headless Service has clusterIP: None: no proxy, only DNS records pointing at Pods. With a StatefulSet (serviceName set), each Pod gets pod.service.namespace.svc.cluster.local. Names stay stable while Pod IPs change. Create the headless Service before the StatefulSet."}],"n":29,"slug":"29-headless-service"},{"title":"Gateway API","lead":"Gateway API is the modern way to bring outside traffic into a cluster. It fixes the two big problems of Ingress: very limited features and no clear split between platform and app teams. You'll learn its three resources and try weighted routing.","sections":[{"h":"","id":"","t":"This lesson assumes you know what Ingress and ingress controllers are, and what a Service is. Gateway API resources are CRDs, which is why they can be installed and updated separately from Kubernetes."},{"h":"The story so far: north-south traffic","id":"s1","t":"Traffic inside the cluster (east-west, service to service) is easy because Kubernetes has its own network. Traffic from the outside world into the cluster (north-south) is harder. Kubernetes does not have a built-in load balancer. Its answer was Ingress. Load-balancer makers (NGINX, HAProxy, cloud providers) write an ingress controller. The cluster admin installs it once. App teams then create Ingress resources and the controller builds the load balancer. It worked, but years of use revealed two drawbacks."},{"h":"Drawback 1: Ingress can say very little","id":"s2","t":"An Ingress can express only: \"which Service\" and \"on which host or path\". Real production needs more: rate limiting, a web application firewall, redirects, URL rewrites, canary (weighted) traffic, and so on. None of these exist in the Ingress spec. Controllers worked around it with annotations. That created new pain: Huge numbers of annotations (30 to 40 for one controller). Some are JSON strings inside YAML, such as weighted routing for AWS ALB. Hard to read and easy to get wrong. Annotations differ between NGINX, ALB and others, so switching controllers means relearning and rewriting."},{"h":"Drawback 2: no separation of roles","id":"s3","t":"In many companies the platform team owns the controller and which class to use, while app teams own routing for their service. With Ingress, both edit the same object, so an app developer can accidentally change what the platform team set up. The community asked to split Ingress into parts. Changing Ingress itself would break everyone, so Kubernetes created something new."},{"h":"Gateway API: three resources, three jobs","id":"s4","t":"Gateway API solves the same problem as Ingress (north-south traffic), but split into three resources: Resource What it says Who owns it GatewayClass Which controller implementation to use (like an IngressClass) Platform / cluster admin Gateway The entry point: which class, and which ports and protocols to listen on Platform or app team (via RBAC) HTTPRoute Routing rules: hosts, paths, backend Services, weights, filters App / DevOps team Each resource has its own owner, so teams can't step on each other. Problem 1 solved: HTTPRoute has real fields for weights, redirects, header matches and rewrites. No more giant annotations. Problem 2 solved: three separate resources means you can give each team RBAC permission on only the ones it owns (see Lesson 11). It's also portable: the same resources work across controllers (Envoy Gateway, NGINX Gateway Fabric, Traefik, cloud implementations). NoteRate limiting, WAF and similar features are still provided by the controller, often through extra policy resources it defines. Gateway API gives a standard place and shape for the common ones (redirects, rewrites, weights, header rules) instead of annotation strings."},{"h":"Hands-on: from zero to a routed app","id":"s5","t":"This demo uses a local cluster (see kind) and Envoy Gateway, a beginner-friendly controller. Step 1: platform engineer installs the controller The Helm chart installs the Gateway API CRDs and the controller in one go (see Lesson 27). Check the Envoy Gateway docs for the current version number. kind create cluster --name gw-demo helm install eg oci://docker.io/envoyproxy/gateway-helm \\ --version <version> -n envoy-gateway-system --create-namespace kubectl get pods -n envoy-gateway-system kubectl get crds | grep gateway You should see gatewayclasses, gateways and httproutes CRDs. The controller now waits for Gateway and HTTPRoute objects, just as an ingress controller waits for Ingress objects. Step 2: deploy an app with a ClusterIP Service Any app will do. The demo app is a small \"echo\" server that replies with request details, exposed on port 3000. kubectl create deployment backend --image=<your-echo-image> kubectl expose deployment backend --port=3000 Step 3: GatewayClass (platform) apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: GatewayClass metadata: name: eg spec: controllerName: gateway.envoyproxy.io/gatewayclass-controller This says \"anything using class eg is handled by Envoy Gateway\". Many installs create it for you. Step 4: Gateway (the entry point) apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Gateway metadata: name: eg spec: gatewayClassName: eg listeners: - name: http protocol: HTTP port: 80 gatewayClassName tells the Gateway which controller owns it (like ingressClassName). The listener says: listen on HTTP port 80. The controller creates a proxy Pod and a Service for it. Step 5: HTTPRoute (the routing rules) apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: HTTPRoute metadata: name: backend spec: parentRefs: - name: eg hostnames: - \"www.example.com\" rules: - backendRefs: - name: backend port: 3000 parentRefs attaches the route to the Gateway. Requests with host www.example.com go to the backend Service on port 3000. Step 6: test it A local cluster can't create a cloud load balancer, so tunnel to the proxy's Service with port-forward. Find its name in the controller namespace first. kubectl get svc -n envoy-gateway-system kubectl port-forward -n envoy-gateway-system svc/<envoy-proxy-service> 9090:80 # in a second terminal curl --verbose --header \"Host: www.example.com\" http://localhost:9090/ The reply comes from your backend Pod. The Host header matters: the route only matches that hostname."},{"h":"Two features that used to need annotations","id":"s6","t":"URL rewrite Users call /get, but the app now serves /replace. Rewrite it in the route and nobody notices: rules: - matches: - path: type: PathPrefix value: /get filters: - type: URLRewrite urlRewrite: path: type: ReplacePrefixMatch replacePrefixMatch: /replace backendRefs: - name: backend port: 3000 Weighted routing (canary) With Ingress this was a JSON annotation. Now it is one field per backend: rules: - backendRefs: - name: app-v1 port: 3000 weight: 8 - name: app-v2 port: 3000 weight: 2 Weights are relative, so 8 and 2 mean about 80% and 20%. Deployment strategies are explained in Lesson 18. Try it: weighted routing Set the weights of app-v1 and app-v2, then send 100 requests through the Gateway. v1 weight 8 v2 weight 2 Interview angle\"Why Gateway API over Ingress?\" Richer routing built into the spec (no annotation sprawl), portable across controllers, and role-oriented design: GatewayClass for platform admins, Gateway and HTTPRoute for app teams."},{"h":"Check yourself","id":"s7","t":"Which resource does an application team normally write to define paths, hosts and backends? HTTPRoute holds the routing rules. GatewayClass is chosen by the platform team. What was a main complaint about Ingress that Gateway API fixes? Ingress spec is small, so features moved into annotations, and one object mixed platform and app concerns. How does an HTTPRoute connect to a Gateway? parentRefs names the Gateway the route attaches to."},{"h":"Key takeaways","id":"s8","t":"Gateway API solves the same north-south traffic problem as Ingress, with fewer limits. Three resources: GatewayClass (which controller), Gateway (entry point), HTTPRoute (rules). Real fields replace annotation strings: weights, rewrites, redirects, header matching. Role separation: platform owns the class, app teams own routes, enforced with RBAC. Install a controller first; locally, use port-forward to reach the proxy. Resources are CRDs, so they evolve independently of Kubernetes releases."}],"n":30,"slug":"30-gateway-api"}];
